/* BULIG Level 6 · Graded Reading Comprehension — passages, questions and keys from the Division of Bukidnon modules (Grades 7–10). */
window.M = window.M || {}; M.HUB_L6 = {"7":[{"id":"L6-7-0","label":"Pre-test","title":"How a Dog’s Life Was Saved","focus":"Mixed","p":["Bernie, a police dog, loves Boy Scouts and he really has a very good reason for doing so. He would have died were it not for the training of two Boy Scouts who knew how to make a tourniquet. These two boys who were twins were members of the Narra Troop Number 64.","The two boys were on their way home from school. They were busy talking about the Boy Scout Jamboree to be held in Japan. They were lucky to be among the Boy Scouts to represent the Philippines in the jamboree.","“Look!” cried Nick, one of the twins. “See the blood trail on the sidewalk.”","Because Boy Scouts are trained to be of help in time of trouble, they followed the trail. It led them to the backyard of a certain Mr. Gana. They heard a feeble whine. Behind the pile of boxes they found Mr. Gana’s dog, Bernie. He seemed very weak due to loss of blood and looked completely exhausted as he lay.","Bernie greeted the two boys with a feeble wag of his tail. The two boys had hopes of saving his life. If only they could stop the bleeding of his wounded foot.","“I will get my handkerchief,” said Pete reaching into his pocket. “Do you have something to bind it with, Nick?”","The boys did not waste any time. Nick pulled a lead pencil out of his pocket. Pete tore a strip about two or three inches wide from the handkerchief. He folded it over and over three or four times making a strong narrow band. Pete tied the two ends together while Nick held Bernie. Then Pete skipped the band over the dog’s injured foot. Bernie bore the pain without wincing as the two boys tightened the tourniquet.","When the bleeding stopped, the boys went to Mr. Gana and told him what had happened to Bernie. Mr. Gana returned home as soon as possible and found Bernie’s wound well taken care of.","The boys then tried to find out how it all happened. They followed the trail back to where Bernie met the accident. Apparently, Bernie had accidentally stepped on a broken bottle.","Bernie would have bled to death had it not been for the two boys and their tourniquet. This is why Bernie has an excellent reason for loving the Boy Scouts. He never fails to lick Pete’s and Nick’s hands whenever they pass by their home. The twins had a special place in Bernie’s heart."],"q":[["Where was the Boy Scout Jamboree that Nick and Pete were selected to attend?",["The Philippines","Japan","The United States","Mr. Gana’s backyard"],"B","Literal"],["What two specific items did the twins use to stop Bernie’s bleeding?",["A bandage and a stick","A handkerchief and a lead pencil","A piece of string and a ruler","A shoelace and a pen"],"B","Literal"],["Why did the boys choose to follow the blood trail instead of going straight home?",["They were curious to see if there was a crime","They wanted to find a lost pet for a reward","Their training taught them to provide help during trouble","They were looking for a broken bottle"],"C","Interpretive"],["What does the \"feeble wag\" of Bernie’s tail suggest about his condition when the boys found him?",["He was energetic and ready to play","He was extremely weak but still friendly or hopeful","He was angry that the boys entered the yard","He was trying to lead them to the broken bottle"],"B","Interpretive"],["How would you evaluate the twins’ decision to use a lead pencil in their medical intervention?",["It was a mistake because pencils are too fragile for medical use","It was unnecessary because the bleeding would have stopped on its own","It was a resourceful use of available tools to create a functional tourniquet","It was a lucky guess that didn't actually help stop the blood"],"C","Critical"],["What is the author’s primary purpose in mentioning that Bernie is a \"police dog\"?",["To explain why Bernie was so aggressive toward the twins","To show that the Boy Scouts were breaking the law","To highlight the irony that a dog trained to help others needed to be helped himself","To suggest that Bernie was injured while catching a criminal"],"C","Critical"],["Which statement best analyzes the reliability of the boys' scout training as presented in the story?",["The training was outdated because they didn't have a real medical kit","The training was highly effective as it provided life-saving skills for emergency situations","The training was only useful because the boys were twins","The training was focused more on travel than on first aid"],"B","Critical"],["If Nick and Pete had not found a pencil in their pockets, which of these items from a student's bag would be the best substitute to tighten the tourniquet?",["A single sheet of notebook paper","A sturdy wooden ruler","A soft rubber eraser","A small piece of scotch tape"],"B","Creative"],["Based on the ending of the story, what is the most likely way Bernie would react if he saw Nick and Pete in a large crowd of people?",["He would likely seek them out specifically to show affection","He would ignore them because there are too many people","He would bark at them to warn them of more broken glass","He would hide because he associates them with the pain of the tourniquet"],"B","Creative"],["Imagine the \"Narra Troop 64\" wanted to create a commemorative badge for the twins. What symbol would most accurately represent this specific rescue?",["A picture of a school building","A map of Japan","A handkerchief tied around a pencil","A broken bottle on a sidewalk"],"C","Creative"]]},{"id":"L6-7-1","label":"Activity 1","title":"The Most Destructive Creatures on Earth","focus":"Literal","p":["Rats are probably the cleverest and the most destructive animals on earth. These animals can survive anywhere. They have also threatened man's life; rat-borne diseases have killed more people than all the wars in history.","Most of the 650 species of rats live in forests, fields, and jungles. But a species tries to link their destinies with men. They go where men go, live where they live, eat what they eat, and learn their ways.","There are two species of common house rats - the brown rat and the black rat. The brown rat has small ears and thick tail that is shorter than its body. It may weigh as much as one-half kilogram and measure nine inches in length, minus its tail. The brown rat is a burrower. It can squeeze through the sewers for miles and enter a man's home through the plumbing. The black rat is very much smaller than the brown rat.","It has an average weight of one-fourth kilogram. It has large eyes and a tail longer than its body. The black rat, clambers up walls, ropes, and wires. Rats are social animals and live in colonies. They wage wars against others and an \"outsider\" is killed by torture known only to rats. The average life of a rat is nine months, although some may live up to two years. They are very prolific.","It has been estimated that a pair of rats, if allowed to multiply unmolested, could produce up to 350 million descendants in three years.","Everywhere the rats go, they spread contamination. which is a continuing health menace. They carry as many as 35 different diseases. Their fleas spread bubonic plague and typhus. These two diseases have killed more than 200 million people in for countries.","The rat's habits take them from sewer to warehouse to home, spreading disease germs every step of the way. They leave droppings everywhere they go and contaminate everything because in their blood and intestines are organisms that cause dysentery, tularemia, rabies, and many other contagious killer diseases.","Nature has endowed rats with special characteristics to help them survive. They avoid poison but after one or two members had felt its effects. They leave their urine on everything that poison-tainted as a \"keep-off\" sign to other rats.","But there is one way rats become useful to man; Man makes use of rats in his studies of drugs, medicine, and the treatment of disease."],"q":[["According to the text, which physical feature belongs specifically to the black rat?",["A thick tail that is shorter than its body","Small ears and a weight of one-half kilogram","Large eyes and a tail that is longer than its body","A body length of nine inches minus the tail"],"C",null],["What is the average lifespan of a rat as mentioned in the passage?",["Three years","Nine months","Two years","Twelve months"],"B",null],["If left alone to multiply, how many descendants can a single pair of rats produce in three years?",["650 million","200 million","350 million","35 million"],"C",null],["How many different diseases are rats known to carry?",["200 different diseases","650 different diseases","As many as 35 different diseases","Exactly two: bubonic plague and typhus"],"C",null],["According to the final section of the text, how are rats useful to humans?",["They help clean sewers and plumbing","They protect forests and jungles from other animals","They are used in the study of drugs and medicine","They help humans track contaminated food"],"C",null]]},{"id":"L6-7-2","label":"Activity 2","title":"Coconut, the Tree of Life","focus":"Literal","p":["The coconut is a tropical palm. It provides man with almost everything; food, clothing, shelter, home furnishings, fuel, and many others. On the products of the coconut tree, one can subsist almost fully.","The coconut grows palm leaf every month. Each leaf bears a cluster of flowers which mature into clusters of fruits. High at the top of the crown of leaves is the coconut heart or bud. If the heart or the bud is removed, the tree dies. The heart itself makes a tasty treat, a \"millionaire's salad.\"","The unopened flowers are protected by a tough sheath that looks like burlap. From this sheath may be fashioned our shoes, caps, and even a kind of helmet.","Sugar may be obtained from the sap of the unopened flowers. A gallon of sap may be gathered and boiled to a good syrup which can be crystallized into rich, dark sugar. Or if left standing, the sap ferments quickly into a robust beer-like drink called \"tuba\" in the Philippines, \"toddy\" in India, \"tuwak\" in Indonesia. If left alone for a few weeks; the sap becomes fine vinegar.","The nut itself is covered by a thick, packed mass of fibers called \"coir\" which can be woven into strong twine or rope. Coir is also used in padding upholstery. The shell of the coconut is hard, fine-grained substance which can be carved into all kinds of objects; cups, ashtrays, button, buckles, and many others. The charcoal from shells is used as air filters in gas masks, submarines, and cigarette tips.","Coconut water contains sugar, minerals, and vitamins, The nut meat is eaten raw or made into salad; coconut milk is used in cooking food. Heated, it yields oil for cooking, lamp fuel, lotions, pomades, and soaps.","Grated nut meats are made into candies, cakes, and pies. Dried coconut meat or \"copra\" is processed for oil and the residue is made into animal food.","There are many uses of the coconut oil. It is used in shampoos, soaps, toothpaste, lotion, lubricants, hydraulic fluid, paints, synthetic rubber, ice cream, plastics, and others. It is also found in breakfast tables - \"margarine\" as a substitute for butter with all the needed nutrients and vitamins.","There are plenty of uses for the rest of the tree. The fronds are woven into mats and house furnishings, the midribs made into brooms and brushes. Even the roots can be made into dyes.","No other tree on earth is as useful and as the coconut!"],"q":[["According to the text, what will happen to a coconut tree if its heart or bud is removed?",["It will produce more fruit.","It will grow a new leaf every month.","The tree will die.","It will grow a tougher sheath."],"C",null],["What is the name of the beer-like drink made from fermented sap in the Philippines?",["Toddy.","Tuwak.","Tuba.","Copra."],"C",null],["Which part of the coconut tree can be used to create charcoal for air filters in gas masks?",["The roots.","The midribs.","The coir fibers.","The shells."],"D",null],["What is \"coir\" specifically defined as in the passage?",["A thick, packed mass of fibers covering the nut.","The dried meat of the coconut used for oil.","The tough sheath that protects unopened flowers.","The residue left over after processing oil."],"A",null],["Which specific part of the coconut tree is used to make brooms and brushes?",["The roots.","The shells.","The midribs.","The fronds."],"C",null]]},{"id":"L6-7-3","label":"Activity 3","title":"Prison Without Walls","focus":"Interpretive","p":["In 1904, the Iwahig Penal Colony was built. It was a kind of prison where- in long term prisoners, most of them life. The colony was originally, designed to house an overflow of termers, serve their sentences with comparative freedom. convicts from Manila's old Bilibid Prison.","Its basic purpose was to reform and rehabilitate criminals rather than punish them. It is under the supervision of the superintendent of prisons.","The settlement site was once a 34- square mile of virgin jungle. The first prisoners hacked out roads, prepared the fields and slanted coconuts and other fruit-bearing trees. Through the years, the colony has expanded to cover 160square miles of cultivated land.","Only prisoners with comparatively long sentences are assigned at the Iwahig Penal Colony. Newly arrived inmates are quartered in disciplinary barracks for at least six months. They do not have the same privileges as the old-time colonial. Later, when they are fully indoctrinated with the rules and regulations of the colony as well as their duties and obligations, they are assigned to various projects where they can put their special skills to good use.","Illiterate inmates attend adult education classes handled by teachers who are fellow convicts. The children of inmates are educated in schools and reading centers in the settlement. Medical treatment is available at the prison's hospital.","The inmates sleep in spacious barrack-rooms with no barred windows to prevent them from breaking out. They are free to marry, but must have permission of the Director of Prisons to do so. If married, arrangements could be made from their families to live in the settlement. They are free to go about within the 160-square mile limits of the penal colony. Like their free compatriots, the inmates work for a living from P500 to P1,000 a month by farming, fishing, forestry, animal husbandry, construction, jobs in the coconut and coffee industries, and handicrafts.","The colony's extensive forests provide them with wood which they can sell. They can fish in the coastal waters or raise poultry and meat animals for their consumption. But as inmates, they have to work for the prison industries, too, for certain hours and days of the week.","The atmosphere in the colony is quiet good such that some convicts prefer to stay there permanently even when their sentences expired. Deserving Ex-convicts who choose to stay in the colony permanently are granted small plots of land for their use. Thus, social derelicts are turned useful citizens."],"q":[["Why does the author refer to the Iwahig Penal Colony as a \"prison without walls\"?",["Because the jungle was too thick to build actual stone walls.","Because the prisoners had to destroy the walls to plant coconut trees.","Because the prisoners live with \"comparative freedom\" and sleep in rooms without barred windows.","Because the colony is so large that walls would be too expensive to build."],"C",null],["What is the primary reason new inmates are kept in disciplinary barracks for their first six months?",["To punish them for their crimes before they are allowed to see their families.","To ensure they understand the colony’s rules and are prepared for the responsibilities of their new roles.","To wait for an opening in the adult education classes handled by other convicts.","To determine if they have the special skills needed for the coffee industry."],"B",null],["What can you infer about the colony's attitude toward the families of the inmates?",["The colony believes that keeping families together helps in the rehabilitation and reform of the prisoners.","The colony uses families as a way to make sure prisoners work harder in the fields.","The colony only allows families to stay if the prisoners pay a high monthly fee.","The colony views families as a distraction from the adult education classes."],"A",null],["Based on the description of the colony’s activities (farming, fishing, and handicrafts), what is the most likely way the colony prepares inmates for life after prison?",["By teaching them practical job skills and a sense of responsibility so they can support themselves.","By making sure they stay within the 160-square mile limit at all times.","By forcing them to work for the prison industries without any pay.","By encouraging them to stay in the jungle forever instead of returning to the city."],"A",null],["Why do some convicts choose to stay in the colony permanently even after their sentences have expired? ",["They are afraid they will be sent back to Manila’s old Bilibid Prison. ","They have found a peaceful, productive life there and have been granted land to call their own. ","They are not allowed to leave the 160-square mile limits of the penal colony. ","They are required to stay until they finish their adult education classes."],"B",null]]},{"id":"L6-7-4","label":"Activity 4","title":"A Mother's Wisdom","focus":"Interpretive","p":["Barbara was 10 when she was found her first real \"pal\" among the girls in school. Their friendship became all-important to Barbara, and being of a possessive nature, she found herself violently jealous of her friend's interests which did not include Barbara. She couldn't conceal this attitude until her friend became annoyed with her possessiveness. Barbara's mother understood what was happening. She waited for an occasion to talk to Barbara about it without hurting her. One day, Barbara and her mother were admiring some baby chicks. Barbara picked up one and held it so tightly that it almost smothered. It struggled to escape from Barbara's hold. Casually her mother said, \"If you hold a chick too tightly, it wants only to get away. Try holding one gently.\"","The next chick nestled quietly in Barbara's open palm. Her mother said, \"You know, dear, people are the same as chicks. If we hold those whom we love too closely to us, they'll struggle for freedom. Hold them with open hands and they won't feel smothered.\"","Barbara understood the message in her mother's words. Whenever she felt jealous of her friend's attention to others, she always pictures the contented chick nestled in her open palm.","Joseph, Barbara's brother was eight. Being young and mischievous, he used to get into jams and then run to his mother for help to get him out of trouble. But Mother never interfered. She would let him untangle his troubles by himself. Joseph never understood why Mother always declined to help him with his problems.","One day, Joseph saw a cocoon of a moth throbbing and heaving. Squealing with anticipation, Joseph began to open the cocoon with his pocket knife to allow the moth to emerge more easily. To his disappointment, the moth did not fly; it fell off from the twig on which the cocoon hung, then died that very same moment.","Joseph's mother shook him on the shoulders and said, \"Son, the struggle of the moth inside the cocoon was for a purpose, to enable it to gain strength to rise above its surroundings. You did not allow it to mature fully, and when you forcibly opened the cocoon, it can neither walk nor fly. It's the same with children. When a boy like you always has somebody to solve his problems for him, he has no chance to develop, Each of the struggles that we go through on our own builds up our strength to meet the next.\" Barbara and Joseph never forget the lessons they learned from their mother."],"q":[["What did the mother mean when she told Barbara to hold those she loved with \"open hands\"?",["Barbara should literally keep her hands open whenever she is around her friends.","Barbara should give her friends expensive gifts to keep them happy.","Barbara should give her friends the freedom to have other interests instead of being possessive.","Barbara should stop being friends with people who make her feel jealous."],"C",null],["In the story of the moth, what does the \"struggle\" inside the cocoon represent for a person's life?",["The physical pain someone feels when they get into trouble.","The difficult challenges people face that help them grow stronger and more independent.","The time a person spends waiting for someone else to help them solve a problem.","The act of hiding away from the world when things get too hard."],"B",null],["Why did the mother refuse to \"interfere\" or help Joseph when he got into trouble?",["She was too busy watching the baby chicks with Barbara.","She wanted to punish him for being mischievous and getting into \"jams\".","She knew that solving his problems for him would prevent him from developing his own strength.","She did not believe that an eight-year-old was old enough to have real problems."],"C",null],["What can we infer about the moth's death after Joseph opened the cocoon?",["The moth died because it missed the necessary process of gaining strength through its own efforts.","The moth died because Joseph used a pocket knife that was too sharp.","The moth died because it was not mature enough to see the sunlight.","The moth died because it fell off the twig before it could learn how to walk."],"A",null],["How did the mother’s teaching style most likely affect Barbara and Joseph’s future?",["It made them fear their mother because she wouldn't help them.","It provided them with mental \"pictures\" and lessons that helped them handle their own emotions and problems.","It encouraged them to spend more time outside with nature instead of going to school.","It taught them that they should never ask for help, even in an emergency."],"B",null]]},{"id":"L6-7-5","label":"Activity 5","title":"The Miracles of the Fire","focus":"Critical","p":["During the time of Elias, many of the children of Israel honored false gods and false prophets.","One day, Elias said to the Jews, \"Call together all the people of Israel to Mount Carmel. Bring also the prophets of Baal\" (Baal was the god of the pagans.","When all the people gathered together, Elias said, \"If the Lord is God, follow Him. If Baal is God, follow him.\"","The people did not say a word. Elias continued, \"I am the only prophet of the Lord. Baal has four hundred and fifty prophets. Now, let us see who is God. Let the prophets of Baal prepare a sacrifice. We shall dress the animals and put unlighted wood under them. Let the prophets of Baal call upon their god. I shall call on my Lord. God shall answer by fire.\" The people all agreed.","\"O, Baal, hear us!\" This was the prayer of the prophets of Baal. But their prayers were not answered.","Then Elias prayed to the one true God. Fire came down from heaven. The people fell on their faces. They said, \"The Lord is God!\"","The Lord was always with Elias. Elias talked to the children of Israel. He wanted them to remember the Lord, their God.","One day the Lord said to Elias. \"You shall make Eliseus a prophet.\"","Elias found Eliseus in the field. Eliseus left his father and mother and followed Elias. Eliseus was very kind to Elias. He served him and helped him. Elias and Eliseus went from place to place reminding the people of the one true God.","One day, Eliseus and Elias were talking together. A fiery chariot drawn by a fiery horse came between them. A strong wind carried Elias up to heaven. Eliseus watch until he could see Elias no more.","After Elias was brought to heaven in a chariot of fire, his spirit rested upon Eliseus.","The sons of the prophet at Jericho came to meet Eliseus and bowed down before him. Nevertheless, they organized a searching party of fifty men to look for Elias' body on the valleys and mountains but failed to find him. They returned to Jericho to see Eliseus and they decided to remain there to heal the soil and purify the waters for drinking. Eliseus performed a miracle by using water for healing.","One day, Eliseus was leaving Jericho for Bethel, a group of children came out of the city and mocked him. Eliseus turned back and cursed the in the name of the Lord. Suddenly, two bears came out of the wilderness and fore the forty-two boys apart."],"q":[["Why did Elias propose the specific contest of calling down fire from heaven to the prophets of Baal?",["Because he wanted to show off his magical powers to the people of Israel.","Because it was a tradition for prophets to play with fire on Mount Carmel.","To provide undeniable proof of who the one true God was through a physical demonstration.","To see if the four hundred and fifty prophets were faster at preparing animals than he was."],"C",null],["Which statement best evaluates the behavior of the people of Israel when Elias first asked them to choose between God and Baal?",["Their silence suggests they were indecisive or afraid to take a side until they saw a miracle.","Their silence shows they were very respectful of Elias and didn't want to interrupt.","Their silence proves that they did not understand the language Elias was speaking.","Their silence indicates they had already decided to follow Baal."],"A",null],["What is the author’s primary purpose for including the detail about the \"fiery chariot\" taking Elias to heaven?",["To explain how ancient people traveled long distances.","To describe a new type of weather phenomenon involving wind and fire.","To emphasize Elias’s special status as a prophet who was directly honored by God.","To show why Eliseus was left alone in the field."],"C",null],["How does the story justify the harsh punishment of the children who mocked Eliseus at the end of the text?",["It suggests that bears are naturally aggressive animals that live near Bethel.","It implies that Eliseus was a very angry man who did not like children.","It reinforces the theme that disrespecting a prophet—and by extension, God—is a grave offense with serious consequences.","It shows that the children were trying to steal the prophet’s spirit."],"C",null],["What can we conclude about Eliseus's character based on his decision to heal the soil and purify the water at Jericho?",["He was more interested in being a farmer than being a prophet.","He used the power given to him to perform miracles that benefited the community and improved their lives.","He wanted to show the fifty men in the searching party that they should have stayed home.","He was trying to prove that water is more powerful than the fire Elias used."],"B",null]]},{"id":"L6-7-6","label":"Activity 6","title":"Lightning and Thunder","focus":"Critical","p":["Lightning is the same thing as light in an electric bulb. It is electricity. A flash of lightning has twenty times as much electricity as the world uses in 24 hours.","Electricity is always present in the air. When moisture in the air condenses on a tiny grain of dust, the little drop becomes charged with electricity. Then, when the air gets cold and the drops go together to make raindrops, some of the electricity cannot stay on the drops and has to get off. When millions of drops of water are pushing off little bits of electricity, they all go together and make one big flash of lightning.","Flashes of lightning usually go from one cloud to another, they sometimes hit the ground. Lightning is hot. When it hits a house, the later gets burned. When it strikes a tree, the heat turns the tree's sap into steam which must escape quickly, and thus the tree splits open. It may hit a wire fence and lightning travels along the wire just as electricity travels along a write to a lamp.","To protect tall buildings from lightning, lightning rods are put into the ground at the side of the building. People used to think that the rod attracts the lightning and when it strikes the rod, the rod carries the lightning through the wire into the ground. Actually, the rod helps to make the lightning strike somewhere else. The rod allows the ground electricity to come up slowly through the rod and into the air. Lightning does not strike in a place where other electricity comes out slowly.","The safest place during a thunderstorm is in the house. Open windows do not attract lightning as people used to think.","Out of doors, it is not safe to stay under a tree for trees are often struck by lightning. In open fields, the safest thing to do during a thunderstorm is to lie flat on the ground.","Lightning does not make any sound. It makes a big flash like a million lamps put together. Along the flash comes a long roll of noise which we call thunder. It is sometimes so far off that it is just faint mutter; at other times it is so close overhead that it sounds like cannons firing.","Thunder sometimes frighten people although it cannot do any harm. When lightning strikes, air becomes heated so quickly that it explodes into thunder.","Lightning and thunder seldom do any damage. There is no reason for people to be afraid of them."],"q":[["What is the author’s primary goal in the section discussing lightning rods?",["To convince readers that lightning rods are the only way to save a building.","To explain the history of how electricity was discovered in the ground.","To correct a common misconception about how the rods actually function.","To warn people that lightning rods are dangerous because they attract lightning."],"C",null],["Which of the following statements from the text is a scientific fact used to disprove a popular \"myth\" or fear?",["\"Thunder sometimes frighten people although it cannot do any harm.\"","\"Lightning and thunder seldom do any damage.\"","\"Open windows do not attract lightning as people used to think.\"","\"Lightning does not strike in a place where other electricity comes out slowly.\""],"C",null],["How does the author support the claim that \"there is no reason for people to be afraid\" of lightning and thunder?",["By explaining the scientific causes of these events and noting that they rarely cause damage.","By stating that thunder is just a \"faint mutter\" and can never be loud.","By proving that lightning always stays in the clouds and never hits the ground.","By telling readers that they are 100% safe as long as they stay near a wire fence."],"A",null],["Based on the text's explanation of why trees split open, what is the logical cause-and-effect relationship described?",["The weight of the electricity is too heavy for the branches to hold.","The wind from the thunder pushes the tree over.","The extreme heat of the lightning turns the tree's sap into steam, causing it to expand and burst.","The tree tries to escape the lightning by opening its trunk."],"C",null],["What is the author’s purpose in comparing a flash of lightning to \"a million lamps put together\"?",["To suggest that lightning could be used to power city streetlights.","To explain that lightning is made of glass and wire.","To use a familiar object to help the reader visualize the incredible brightness and power of a flash.","To argue that people should turn off their lamps during a thunderstorm."],"C",null]]},{"id":"L6-7-7","label":"Activity 7","title":"The Great Chinese Teacher","focus":"Critical","p":["Confucius' teachings had left an indelible mark in the lives of millions of Chinese.","Thousands of years ago in a flowery kingdom in the state of Lu lived a soldier Known as Kung, the Tall. He was so-called because he was seven feet tall. When he reached his seventieth birthday he reflected sorrowfully over his life. He had nine children and all of them were girls. He had no son to carry on his family name and to perform the rites and ceremonies of ancestors worship. This was practiced by the Chinese throughout the centuries to show their reverence to their forefathers. Kung, the Tall came from a noble lineage and he would not want to break this tradition.","Two years before his death, a son was born to him. His son, named Confucius, became known as a \"Great Sage\" and philosopher to China and the world beyond China. His words and wisdom have such a vast influence on the thoughts and on the ways of life of the Chinese throughout five centuries. He was called Master King.","The Chinese already lived in beautiful houses with fine furniture, grew crops, used agricultural implements and spun silk for their clothing. They used coins, marked time by the moon and had developed cooking to a fine taste. They already had law courts and schools where history, music and literature were taught. They wrote books by hand on bamboo and they bound the pages together with thongs.","Master Kung was a scholar in school. He had two special interests: first, how people should live and conduct themselves; and second, how the state should be governed. When he grew up to manhood he traveled all over the civilized world with a band of disciples. Two or three times, he was given positions of importance in the government but he never stayed long in these positions. The officers in the government resented Master Kung's honesty. Confucius continued teaching not knowing that his ideas were to be passed on from generation to generation, that thousands of books were to be written about him and his sayings, that he was to become one of the most beloved and revered men in history.","He preached the importance the importance of courtesy, orderliness, loyalty, justice, and devotion to one's parents. He emphasized that the government should be governed by men who possessed these virtues. He stressed the importance of happiness in family life and of endurance and consideration for one's neighbors.","These beautiful thoughts had become part of the national character of the Chinese people. They were taught that happiness can be found in cultivating the art of living and in calm and settled existence.","Confucius' greatest influence in Chinese life was family reverence and bonds of family life. He always believed that reverence for one's ancestors was important and desirable."],"q":[["Based on the passage, what was the most likely reason the government officers \"resented Master Kung's honesty\"?",["They were jealous that he was the son of a famous seven-foot-tall soldier.","They did not believe that a teacher should be allowed to hold a position of importance.","His honest nature and high moral standards likely exposed or interfered with their own less-virtuous ways of governing.","They felt that his focus on music and literature was a waste of the government's time."],"C",null],["Which of the following statements from the text is an expression of Confucius’s philosophy (opinion/belief) rather than a historical fact?",["\"They wrote books by hand on bamboo and they bound the pages together with thongs.\"","\"Two or three times, he was given positions of importance in the government.\"","\"Happiness can be found in cultivating the art of living and in calm and settled existence.\"","\"He had nine children and all of them were girls.\""],"C",null],["Why does the author include the detail about Kung, the Tall having nine daughters before finally having a son?",["To show that Kung, the Tall was a very successful and wealthy man.","To explain the cultural importance of carrying on a family name and tradition, which sets the stage for Confucius’s own teachings on family.","To suggest that Confucius had a difficult childhood growing up with nine sisters.","To prove that soldiers in ancient China were required to have large families."],"B",null],["What is the author’s primary purpose in mentioning that the Chinese already lived in beautiful houses and used coins before Confucius began teaching?",["To establish that Confucius lived in a highly developed and civilized society, making his intellectual influence even more impressive.","To argue that the Chinese people did not actually need a teacher because they were already successful.","To explain why Confucius decided to travel the world instead of staying at home.","To show that Confucius was born into a very poor family compared to his neighbors."],"A",null],["How does the author evaluate the long-term impact of Confucius’s life?",["By focusing on the fact that he failed to stay in government positions for a long time.","By describing the specific way he bound bamboo pages together.","By highlighting how his \"beautiful thoughts\" became an \"indelible mark\" on the national character of millions of people.","By proving that he was the only person in the state of Lu who knew how to write."],"C",null]]},{"id":"L6-7-8","label":"Activity 8","title":"Mohammedanism, the Islam Faith","focus":"Creative","p":["Have you ever wondered why our Muslim brothers in Mindanao have a different religion than ours? For a time, Mohammedanism, or the Muslim religion, threatened all Asia and even a great part of Europe. Here's how Mohammedanism came about.","The Mohammedan religion was founded by Mohammed and for a lime was considered the most powerful religion in the world. There are still two hundred thirty million believers of Mohammedan faith in Asia, Africa, and a group in the Philippines, in Mindanao.","Mohammed, the founder of this religion, was born in Mecca in the years 570 A.D. His parents were poor and he received very little education. While still a young boy he worked as a shepherd. At the age of 25, he married a rich widow older than himself.","When he was about 40 years old, the Angel Gabriel appeared to him in one of his prayers. The angel declared him as the prophet of God and told him to preach the true religion.","Mohammed began to preach. His work was slow. He had only 40 followers after four years of preaching but he continued his work. He wrote verses telling about the divinity of God and how mercy could be gained through prayers, fasting, and alms- giving. These verses could be found at the beginning of the Koran, the sacred book of the Mohammedans.","Mohammed was ever patient and never grew tired of preaching his religion, He saw to it that his followers did not eat certain food. Later, he prohibited them from drinking wine, gambling, and indulging is usury, His followers lived by raiding caravans in the deserts and very often they were Successful. Many people of Mecca joined the band of Mohammed.","Then came the years of misunderstanding and strife, War broke out between the followers of Mohammed and the people of Mecca who did not believe in him, Mohammed and his followers attacked Mecca and captured the city in 630 A.D. The people rejoiced and honored him after his victory.","They considered him a prince and God's prophet on earth. He made many conquests after this. His followers swept towards Asia and Africa, and for a time, it was feared that Mohammedanism will succeed to engulf the whole world.","However, Christianity triumphed in many places, and only some countries remained Mohammedans — including the Muslims in Mindanao."],"q":[["If you were an advisor to Mohammed during his first four years of preaching when he only had 40 followers, what creative strategy based on his character might you suggest to help his message reach more people?",["Suggest he give up preaching and return to being a shepherd.","Suggest he continue writing his verses to share with other caravan travelers, using his patience to slowly build trust.","Advise him to use his wife’s wealth to buy a larger army immediately.","Tell him to stop writing verses and only focus on raiding caravans for food."],"C",null],["Imagine a new city is being built today based strictly on the rules Mohammed established for his followers. Which of these businesses would likely be missing from that city?",["A public library filled with sacred books.","A school for teaching young children how to pray.","A casino that offers wine and gambling games.","A marketplace for trading silk and spices."],"B",null],["If the Angel Gabriel had not appeared to Mohammed when he was 40, how might his life have most likely continued based on his background before that event?",["He would have become a famous general in the Roman army.","He would have likely continued his life as a member of the merchant class or managed his family's interests in Mecca.","He would have moved to Mindanao to start a new colony.","He would have traveled to Europe to study at a university."],"A",null],["Based on the \"Five Pillars\" logic often associated with the Koran (prayers, fasting, and alms-giving), how would a follower most likely respond to seeing a person in the desert who has no food or water?",["By ignoring them and continuing on their journey to Mecca.","By challenging them to a race for the remaining supplies.","By practicing \"alms-giving\" and sharing their own food and water as an act of mercy.","By telling the person they should have prayed harder before they left."],"C",null],["If you were tasked with creating a modern \"Peace Treaty\" between the people of Mecca and Mohammed’s followers in 630 A.D., which of Mohammed's personal traits mentioned in the text would be most helpful in making the treaty last?",["His ability to lead successful raids on caravans.","His lack of formal education as a young boy.","His \"ever patient\" nature and his focus on mercy and divinity.","His victory in capturing the city by force."],"C",null]]},{"id":"L6-7-9","label":"Activity 9","title":"The Ifugaos","focus":"Creative","p":["The Ifugaos inhabiting the Mountain Province of Northern Luzon are among the most interesting people in the Far East. They are direct descendants of the earliest Malay invaders of the Philippines.","They have constructed one of the worlds wonders.","Ifugao men wear clothes or G-strings; the women wear short skirts reaching from waist to the knees, Men and women alike wear ornaments of gold, beads, brass armlets and legless. They build small but strong thatched houses.","The Ifugaos have many interesting customs. Once a girl reaches the age of puberty, she is allowed to send the nights with other girls in the house of some spinster or widow called” (ulog in Bontoc). Young men come to the ayamang at night to express their love, The suitors are rejected or admitted to the ayamang depending upon the reactions of the young maidens.","Trial marriage is practiced in the ayamang, A girl can live with the man she prefers. When she is about to become a mother, arrangements are made between their parents for their marriage.","Divorce may be had any time by either party on the Condition that the dissatisfied party pays a fine to the family of the other. All the property of the divorced spouses go to their children.","Ifugao customs and traditions abound in taboos, It is taboo for a pregnant woman to wear ornaments forming a circle. It is believed that such ornaments will make child delivery difficult, People from one village cannot pass through another village during rice harvest time. A stranger is not allowed to enter a village during its rest- day celebration. Anyone is prohibited to enter an Ifugao house when the owner is absent. A person doing so is considered a thief even if he has not taken anything from the house.","The Ifugaos are lovers of law and order. Trial by ordeal by hot water or the hot bolo test -----is practiced in trials for criminal offenses. The trial is a punishment in itself. As a result, there are few crimes in Ifugao land.","Fines are assessed in accordance with the wealth of the offending party. In this manner, a poor man Would not be required to pay a fine he cannot afford.","We have always thought of the Ifugaos as Uncivilized. In the light of their practices, could we still consider them as a society that achieved extraordinary architectural and social milestones independently."],"q":[["If you were a modern engineer visiting the Ifugao mountains, which aspect of their ancient \"world wonder\" would you study most to help build sustainable farms in other steep mountain regions?",["The specific types of G-strings and skirts worn by the workers.","Their system of stone-walled terraces and irrigation that prevents soil erosion on steep slopes.","The trial by ordeal methods used to settle disputes between villages.","The ornaments of gold and beads worn by the men and women."],"B",null],["Imagine you are a traveler approaching an Ifugao village during their \"rest-day\" celebration. Based on their taboos, what would be the most respectful and \"creative\" way to handle the situation?",["Enter the village anyway and offer gold beads as a peace offering.","Set up a camp outside the village limits and wait until the rest-day is over before attempting to enter.","Try to find a house where the owner is absent so you can rest quietly inside.","Walk through the village quickly while the people are busy with their harvest."],"C",null],["If an Ifugao leader wanted to modernize their \"trial by ordeal\" (hot water or hot bolo test) while still keeping their traditional focus on \"law and order,\" what new method might they create?",["A race to the top of the highest mountain to see who is faster.","A system where the person who pays the most gold is found innocent.","A communal \"Peace Council\" where both parties must perform a community service task together to prove their sincerity.","Eliminating all trials and allowing people to take whatever they want from absent owners."],"B",null],["A pregnant Ifugao woman wants to follow the tradition of avoiding circular ornaments but still wants to look beautiful for a ceremony. Based on your creative understanding of the text, what could she wear instead?",["A heavy circular brass belt that covers her waist.","A necklace made of perfectly round gold beads.","Straight-lined brass armlets or rectangular woven patterns that do not form a complete circle.","A large circular hat made of thatched palm."],"C",null],["If the Ifugao \"trial marriage\" system in the ayamang was adapted into a modern school project about \"responsibility,\" what would be the main lesson students would learn?",["That you should never listen to your parents' advice about marriage.","That big life decisions require a period of testing and mutual agreement between families before they become permanent.","That girls should live alone in the mountains until they become mothers.","That paying a fine is the best way to solve any problem in school."],"C",null]]},{"id":"L6-7-10","label":"Activity 10","title":"Plant Thieves","focus":"Creative","p":["Most plants with green leaves earn their own living. They do not need help from other plants inasmuch as they gather from the soil and air the materials on which they live. Their roots extend deep into the soil seeking out water and the substances the soil supplies.","Moreover, their leaves get carbon dioxide from the air. With the help of sunshine, they are able to manufacture their food. These plants do not destroy other plants or animals or steal from them to obtain their food. These selfsupporting plants live and let other plants live. But some plants are dependent on other plants. They also try to obtain all the food they could by robbing their innocent victims. These plants have no green-leaf substances and could not make for themselves. Such plants are supplied entirely by their victims or hosts as they are usually called.","There are plant thieves that steal from their hosts as much as they can, but because they have green leaves, they manufacture little food for themselves, like all thieves, plant robbers have sneaking ways. They have different ways of attacking their victims, The dodder is a plant that has a tangle of yellow threads. In season, these yellow threads are vine- like sterna twining about the branches of the host plant. The dodder has a bad reputation as a plant thief in the plant world It has no roots nor leaves. Instead, there are minute scales. With no roots nor leaves, the dodder grows very well and bears flowers and seeds.","The secret of its food supply is the slender vines. These thin thread-like stems are firmly fastened by rows of little suckers to the stems and branched of the host plant. The suckers adhere to the host. The dodder sends minute branches right through the bark of its victim into the part where the sap flows. This way, it robs the host of its hard- earned riches supply itself with food for its vigorous growth and for the formation of its cluster of flowers which it shamelessly unfolds over the branches of its victim. The flowers, of course, form abundant seeds to start a new crop of robbers for the next season."],"q":[["If you were an artist creating a \"villain\" character for a comic book based on the dodder plant, which physical feature from the text would be the most accurate \"weapon\" for your character?",["Large green leaves that block the sunlight from others.","Deep, powerful roots that pull all the water out of the ground.","Slender, yellow thread-like arms with rows of \"suckers\" used to rob energy from victims.","Bright, colorful flowers that trick people into coming closer."],"C",null],["Imagine you have a garden where a \"plant thief\" has started to grow on your favorite rosebush. Based on how the dodder grows, what would be the most effective way to save your rosebush for the next season?",["Watering the dodder plant more so it doesn't have to steal from the rose.","Covering the rosebush with a blanket to hide it from the thief.","Carefully removing the dodder before it forms its \"abundant seeds\" to prevent a new crop of robbers from growing later.","Planting more green-leafed plants nearby to teach the dodder how to make its own food."],"C",null],["If the \"self-supporting plants\" and the \"plant thieves\" were human characters in a story about a community, which of these scenarios best represents the behavior of the \"Plant Thieves\"?",["A group of friends who share their snacks and help each other with homework.","A person who never brings their own tools to work and always secretly uses their neighbor's expensive equipment without asking.","A hard-working farmer who grows enough vegetables to feed the entire village.","A scientist who invents a new way to turn sunshine into electricity for everyone."],"B",null],["A scientist discovers a way to give \"green-leaf substances\" (chlorophyll) to a dodder plant through a special injection. Based on the logic of the text, how would the dodder's life most likely change?",["It might become less dependent on its host because it could finally manufacture some of its own food using sunshine.","It would grow even more suckers and kill the host plant twice as fast.","It would lose its yellow color and turn into a tree with deep roots.","It would immediately stop bearing flowers and seeds."],"A",null],["If you were writing a \"Safety Guide for Plants,\" what would be the best advice for a \"host\" plant to avoid being attacked by a \"thief\" like the dodder?",["\"Grow as many green leaves as possible to hide your stem.\"","\"Deepen your roots so the thief cannot reach your water supply.\"","\"Watch out for yellow, vine-like stems twining around your branches, as they want to send suckers through your bark.\"","\"Try to grow only in the shade where the thief cannot find you.\""],"C",null]]},{"id":"L6-7-11","label":"Post-test","title":"Pope John Paul II","focus":"Mixed","p":["Pope John Paul II is Karol Wojtyla who was born on May 18, 1920 to Karol and Emilia Kaszrowska. His mother died when he was nine years old. At the age of 21 his father left him, too.","He graduated at the top of his class when he was 18; began his college studies at the City of Cracow; shifted to Theology in secret during the German occupation, and was ordained priest on November 1, 1946. He was consecrated Bishop on September 28, 1958 and was made an Archbishop on December 30, 1963. Four years later, on May 29, 1967, he became a Cardinal. He then became the Supreme Pontiff a position naming him as the head of the Roman Catholic religion.","Most notable about the Pope is his genuine and sterling love for humanity, his great gearing for peace and love for all men, his keen understanding of human frailties, and most of all, his concern for freedom and human rights.","Pope John Paul II is completely averse to the luxury of pomp and grandeur. He is at home in simple surroundings. Named the Head of the church in Poland, he chose a room of Sparta surroundings. When he became a Pope, he reprimanded the personnel staff who removed his belongings to a more comfortable room during his absence. Briefly, he wanted a simple life, sans the trimmings of a luxurious and kingly life.","On his first day as Supreme Pontiff, Pope John Paul II showed signs of non- confinement within the walls of the Vatican. He disregarded the tradition of seclusion. In less than 24 hours after assumption of office, he was up and about and left the Vatican, much to the concern of the cautious officials, and went to a Roman hospital to pay a visit to an old friend, Polish Bishop Andrzej Maria Deskur. Once more he broke an age-long tradition of talking to a group of hospital officials. As he is desirous to communicate with everyone, he ignores protocol and goes as a politician would, shaking hands and kissing babies.","When he came to the Philippines, he was not a stranger in our minds, but a humble brother seeking to find old and new friends, a shepherd tending a flock of sheep. He came as a leader to the only-Christian nation in the Far East back then, the Philippines.","His message was one of brotherhood, peace, and loving sympathy for the oppressed, the downtrodden, the. unjustly jailed, but most of all intended to families who have a special place in his heart. His death on April 2, 2005 was deeply mourned by all people, Catholic and non-Catholic as well.","He left the legacy of \"Service to others.\""],"q":[["On what date was Karol Wojtyla ordained as a priest?",["May 18, 1920","September 28, 1958","November 1, 1946","May 29, 1967"],"C","Literal"],["Whom did the Pope visit in a Roman hospital less than 24 hours after assuming office?",["His father, Karol","A group of Polish students","Bishop Andrzej Maria Deskur","The President of Poland"],"C","Literal"],["The text mentions that the Pope \"reprimanded the personnel staff\" for moving his belongings to a more comfortable room. What does this action reveal about his character?",["He was a difficult person to please.","He was deeply committed to living a simple life and avoided luxury.","He did not trust the officials working at the Vatican.","He preferred the furniture he had back in Poland."],"B","Interpretive"],["Why does the author describe the Pope as acting \"as a politician would\" when he visited people?",["To illustrate his desire to connect directly with common people through physical gestures like shaking hands.","To suggest that he was planning to run for a government office in Italy.","To show that he followed the strict protocols of the Vatican.","To explain why he was constantly surrounded by cautious officials."],"A","Interpretive"],["What is the author's primary purpose for mentioning that Karol Wojtyla studied Theology \"in secret\" during the German occupation?",["To show that he was a student who enjoyed playing games.","To highlight his courage and dedication to his faith even during dangerous times.","To explain why it took him so long to graduate from college.","To criticize the German educational system of that era."],"B","Critical"],["Which of the following statements from the text represents an opinion or a metaphor used by the author rather than a historical fact?",["\"His mother died when he was nine years old.\"","\"He left the Vatican... and went to a Roman hospital.\"","He was \"a humble brother seeking to find old and new friends, a shepherd tending a flock of sheep.\"","\"His death on April 2, 2005 was deeply mourned by all people.\""],"C","Critical"],["How does the author support the claim that Pope John Paul II showed signs of \"non-confinement within the walls of the Vatican\"?",["By listing his various titles such as Bishop, Cardinal, and Pope.","By providing a specific example of him breaking tradition to leave the Vatican and visit a friend.","By stating that he was born in Poland instead of Italy.","By explaining that he graduated at the top of his class when he was 18."],"B","Critical"],["If Pope John Paul II were to visit a modern school today, which of his core values mentioned in the text would he most likely encourage students to practice?",["The importance of moving to comfortable rooms.","The need to follow strict protocols and traditions.","The legacy of \"Service to others\" and love for humanity.","The pursuit of luxury and kingly trimmings."],"C","Creative"],["Imagine you are a Vatican official responsible for the Pope's safety. Based on the text, what would be your biggest challenge in protecting him?",["His refusal to travel to other countries like the Philippines.","His tendency to ignore protocol and wander into crowds to interact with people.","His preference for secret studies in Cracow.","His insistence on keeping his room in Poland exactly as it was."],"B","Creative"],["Based on the Pope's \"simple life\" philosophy, how would he most likely choose to spend a large donation given to him personally?",["Buying expensive furniture for the Vatican staff.","Using it to help the oppressed, the downtrodden, and families in need.","Building a luxurious summer palace in Poland.","Investing it in a private collection of kingly ornaments."],"B","Creative"]]}],"8":[{"id":"L6-8-0","label":"Pre-test","title":"A Glass of Milk","focus":"Mixed","p":["In a small town, there lived a poor little boy who used to do odd jobs like selling different objects from door to door. The money he earned from those jobs was used to buy his daily food and pay his school expenses. It was a usual day of selling items from house to house when he felt very hungry and sick. He was so famished that he couldn’t even walk any more steps.","With enough strength, he decided to ask for food at a house in the neighborhood. When the poor boy knocked on the door of a house, a beautiful young girl opened the door to his surprise. With much hesitation, he requested the young girl for a glass of water. The boy said, “Can you please give me a glass of water?” The pretty girl was taken aback after seeing the poor boy’s condition and understood how hungry he was.","She said, “Sure, wait a minute.” She went into the kitchen, and instead of a glass of water as requested by the little boy, she brought him a glass of milk. “Here, little boy. Drink it!” said the young girl. The boy was astonished to see the glass of milk in front of him. He drank the milk and felt better than ever. After drinking the milk, the boy thanked the kind-hearted girl and asked her, “How much do I owe you for this milk?”","“Oh dear, I don’t want any money for this”, the girl replied softly. The boy thanked the compassionate girl from the bottom of his heart and then left the place. Years passed by. The young girl grew up into a beautiful grown woman. One day she fell severely ill and was taken to the hospital. There, she had some tests, which unfortunately revealed that she had a rare neurological disorder that required several treatments and great care for many months.","She was hospitalized at one of the city’s best hospitals as her health deteriorated day by day. Dr Kevin, a well-known neuro specialist, was assigned the girl’s case by the hospital. Dr Kevin found the girl’s illness hard to cure despite his extraordinary expertise. However, he did not give up. The treatment took months. With determination and much perseverance, Dr Kevin successfully brought the girl’s ailment under control. The girl was finally cured of the disease after careful observation and medication.","Everyone in the hospital complimented the doctor for his successful work. But the girl had her own worries about the hospital bills. She thought to herself, “How will I pay for such an extensive treatment when I have only a little money of my own?” The girl’s family did not leave her enough money that could possibly clear her extensive treatment and therapy in that reputed hospital. Finally, the bill arrived.","With shaking hands, she opened it and was astonished to see that the bill had been cancelled across. Beneath the amount, there was a note signed by her treatment doctor, Dr Kevin. The note said, “Bill paid in full years ago with a glass of milk!” The girl remembered the young boy she had helped years ago and felt happy."],"q":[["What kind of work did the little boy do to earn money?",["Farming","Delivering milk","Working in a hospital","Selling items from door to door"],"D",null],["What did the young girl give the boy instead of water?",["Juice","Bread","Soup","A glass of milk"],"D",null],["Why did the girl give the boy milk instead of water?",["She did not like giving water","She had no water in the house","She wanted to show off her kindness","She understood that he needed nourishment"],"D","Interpretive"],["What does the note “Bill paid in full years ago with a glass of milk” imply?",["The hospital made a mistake","The girl had already paid the bill before","The doctor did not charge hospital fees","The doctor remembered and repaid her kindness"],"D","Interpretive"],["Was it fair for the doctor to cancel the woman’s hospital bill?",["No, because the girl did not ask for help","Yes, because he was the doctor in charge","No, because hospital services must always be paid","Yes, because kindness should always be repaid"],"D","Critical"],["Which action best shows perseverance in the story?",["The girl worrying about the bill","The girl giving milk to the boy","The boy selling items door to door","The doctor continuing treatment despite difficulties"],"D","Critical"],["What could have happened if the girl had ignored the hungry boy?",["Nothing important would change","The girl would still be cured","The boy would still become a doctor","The story’s message about kindness would be lost"],"D","Critical"],["If the boy had never met the girl again, what message would the story still teach?",["Doctors are always generous","Success comes from wealth","Kindness always brings immediate rewards","Helping others is valuable even without reward"],"D","Creative"],["If the story were told from the girl’s point of view, what emotion would likely stand out at the end?",["Fear","Pride","Gratitude","Confusion"],"C","Creative"],["What modern situation best reflects the message of the story?",["A doctor becoming famous","A patient refusing treatment","A person becoming rich overnight","A student helping a classmate without expecting recognition"],"D","Creative"]],"keyBy":"claude"},{"id":"L6-8-1","label":"Activity 1","title":"A Lost Civilization","focus":"Literal","p":["A long, long time ago, thousands of years before there were cities in any place in the world, wandering hunters from the Asia mainland crossed the narrow channel to Alaska.","These people who were called Indians slowly spread over the two American continents. Some of them remained hunters, the others became farmers and built great cities. From these Indians descended a distinctive group called the Mayas.","The Mayas had cities long before any white man landed in the American continent. By the seventh century A.D. they had built many cities in Central America. The ruins of these cities could still be found standing in the jungles of Central America. The most famous of these cities was called \"Chichen Itza\". In its ruins were found many beautiful buildings, terraces, temples, and a great plaza with many courts. The palaces and monasteries were built for their nobles and priests.","There was even an observatory for studying the stars and their movements and other meteorological researches: The priests carefully watched the movements of the stars. They made a calendar which was as accurate as the one we use today. It was more accurate than the calendars used in Europe and Asia at that time. The Mayas dressed in the fine locks. They wore gold ornaments. Most precious among their ornaments were of jade.","Jades and rich plume feathers were worn only by the rich and the powerful nobles and priests. They used a system of writing similar to the ancient Babylonians using character symbols because they had no alphabet. They wrote books on their history, astronomy, and religion. Many of these books, however, have been lost. Only three are in existence today.","The Mayan engineers made fine roads similar to those built by the Romans at the height of their power. They used layers of limestone fill the roadway and gravel to fill in the crevices. They then covered the surface with fine limestone which acted as a sort of cement. A heavy road-roller hewn by hand out of hard stone was rolled over the road over and over again. The rollers were so heavy. It took 15 to 20 men to work them over the roads. The roads they made were very good. With little repairs, they have been used until recent times.","The farmers among the Mayas lived in villages outside the cities. They raised crops and built roads, temples and palaces, had no wheeled vehicle nor tamed beasts of burden. They used their own muscles to carry heavy loads.","How amazing therefore that they did build magnificent roads and temples and palaces with their own puny hands!"],"q":[["Where did the wandering hunters originally come from before crossing to Alaska?",["Europe","Africa","Asia mainland","South America"],"C",null],["What group descended from the early Indians mentioned in the story?",["Aztecs","Incas","Mayas","Romans"],"C",null],["What was the most famous Mayan city mentioned in the passage?",["Tenochtitlan","Machu Picchu","Palenque","Chichen Itza"],"D",null],["Who were allowed to wear jade and rich plume feathers according to the story?",["Farmers","Hunters","All Mayas","Rich and powerful nobles and priests"],"D",null],["How did the Mayas carry heavy loads since they had no wheeled vehicles or beasts of burden?",["Using carts","Using animals","Using boats","Using their own muscles"],"D",null]],"keyBy":"claude"},{"id":"L6-8-2","label":"Activity 2","title":"I Refuse","focus":"Literal","p":["The life story of Dr. Jose Rizal has been told and retold many, many times that almost all the intimate details of his life are well known to us. But very little has been written of Jose Rizal's mother, Doña Teodora Alonzo. Do many of us know, for example, that she refused to accept pension offered to her by the Philippine government?","Doña Teodora, the mother who gave our nation the neatest Malayan, is a heroine in her own right. She had suffered imprisonment for just being Jose Rizal's mother. She suffered more than any other mother in Philippine history for raising and educating her son. Jose.","The Rizal family had been impoverished because of Dr. Rizal's struggles against Spanish tyranny. Doña Teodora found herself in straightened circumstances years after the death of our hero. Therefore, she rightfully deserved the pension offered her by our government. In 1908, the Philippine Legislature, aware of Doña Teodora's need, unanimously agreed to grant her a life pension as a token of the gratitude of the Filipino people. At that time, she was residing in the house of one of her daughters in Manila.","A delegation of lawmakers was appointed to advise her of the pension. The delegation thanked her for the patriotic service to her country and people and told her that the government was giving her a substantial pension which would make her comfortable and secure during her remaining years.","\"A substantial pension is offered to you as a token of the nation's gratitude for your patriotism, Doña Teodora, \"the chairman of the delegation said.","Doña Teodora firmly but politely refused to accept the pension. \"Our family is not patriotic because of mercenary motives,\" she told the group of delegation. \"If the government has enough money to offer me a life pension, it should give this money to the poor who deserve it more. I have my children and relatives to take care of me,\" Doña Teodora continued.","Thus, the mother of our greatest hero proved herself worthy as a mother of a heroic son. What a contrast to our present-day so-called \"heroes\" and \"patriots\" who not only clamor for pensions and backpays but also, to add insult to injury, claim that they deserve them. Hear howl when action on their application is delayed. They could not wait until their services were given just rewards and recognition.","Doña Teodora's self- abnegation should serve as a lesson to our pension-·hungry and so- called \"heroes\" and patriots who mask their greed in the guise of patriotism."],"q":[["Who is the mother of Jose Rizal mentioned in the story?",["Doña Aurora","Doña Teodora Alonzo","Doña Marcela","Doña Trinidad"],"B",null],["What did the Philippine government offer Doña Teodora in 1908?",["A house","A medal","A life pension","A government position"],"C",null],["Why did Doña Teodora deserve the pension according to the passage?",["She fought in wars","She was wealthy","She worked for the government","Her family had been impoverished because of Rizal’s struggle"],"D",null],["Where was Doña Teodora living when the lawmakers visited her?",["In Calamba","In Spain","In Malacañang Palace","In the house of one of her daughters in Manila"],"D",null],["Why did Doña Teodora refuse the pension?",["She disliked the lawmakers","She planned to leave the country","She had already received another pension","She believed the money should be given to the poor"],"D",null]],"keyBy":"claude"},{"id":"L6-8-3","label":"Activity 3","title":"The Enchanted Trunk","focus":"Interpretive","p":["There once lived a fairy in a big balete tree near the mouth of a cave. She was known as Macaban. Unlike other fairies, she was visible most of the time. She had brown eyes and curly hair. Her voice was sweet and was always kind. She was very generous. She was always ready to help the unfortunate, and she comforted them in their sorrows. She gave food and clothing to the poor.","During wedding, she would lend the bridegroom his wedding attire, precious stones, gold tablewares, kitchen utensils, which she kept in storerooms inside an underground city. Anybody could utter his request at the keyhole; the stone door would open at once to let him in and select the things he needed. The goodness of Macaban was everywhere. The borrowers came not only from Inabangan but also from neighboring towns.","One day, a dishonest couple came to borrow some wedding attire, jewels and tableware for their son's wedding. They were allowed to enter the city storerooms to get the things they needed. Seeing that nobody kept watch, they picked up several precious objects and put them in their bags and pockets. When they went out of the door, they promised the door keeper to return everything after the wedding, but they did not tell him of the jewels that bulged in their pockets thinking that Macaban would never know. After the wedding, the dishonest couple did not return the things they borrowed. Instead, they went around the town and urged the people to destroy the home of Macaban and to drive the fairy away.","Macaban was angry at what the dishonest couple did to her. So, she thought of teaching them a lesson. She called all the people to enter the city and select a gift for themselves. She gave them 20 minutes to select that they liked best on condition that they must not stay more than the given time.","The honest people obeyed the command of Macaban for they stayed in the storeroom for only the time allowed. On the other hand, the dishonest couple went into the heart of the city collecting jewels and precious stones. When the 20minutes were over, Macaban waved her hand. Deep water rushed down the street separating the two high cliffs.","\"Because you are dishonest, you must remain here forever,\" Macaban cried, \"You cannot cross the deep water and escape from the many crocodiles.\" Until today, the keyhole, the mouth of the cave, and the stone door can be seen by boat riders going up and down the Inabangan River in Bohol. People say these parts of the enhanced trunk of Macaban, a splendid city underneath the mountain."],"q":[["Why did Macaban allow anyone to borrow items from her underground city without strict supervision?",["She was careless and forgetful","She wanted people to steal from her","She did not value her possessions","She trusted people and believed in their goodness"],"D",null],["What does the dishonest couple’s behavior reveal about their character?",["They were fearful of Macaban","They were curious and adventurous","They were greedy and ungrateful","They were confused about the rules"],"C",null],["Why did Macaban give the people only 20 minutes to choose their gifts?",["To punish everyone","To protect her city from flooding","To hurry the wedding celebration","To test who would follow instructions"],"D",null],["What does the fate of the dishonest couple suggest about the story’s message?",["Power should be feared","Wealth brings happiness","Clever people always succeed","Dishonesty leads to serious consequences"],"D",null],["What lesson does the story mainly teach readers?",["Fairies should not trust humans","Riches should be shared secretly","Obedience and honesty are rewarded","Fear is the best way to control people"],"C",null]],"keyBy":"claude"},{"id":"L6-8-4","label":"Activity 4","title":"Mutya ng Saging","focus":"Interpretive","p":["It was night after supper. The children were gathered around Lolo Hugo waiting for his nightly treat, a story. Lolo Hugo had a trunk load of stories. He had never been at a loss for a story his grandchildren would love so much to hear; and very often, the children were left in wishful thinking after hearing his story.","\"Tell us the story of Juan Tamad,\" said four-year-old Lito, sitting on his grandfather's lap.","\"We heard that last night,\" interrupted Imelda. \"Tell us about the aswang or the tikbalang please,\" she continued. She always wanted to hear stories that were shivery and hair-raising. Imelda was Lolo Hugo's only granddaughter.","\"Tonight, I'm going to tell you about the \"Mutya ng Saging”. You must listen very carefully because when you grow up, I would like you to look for it. If you find it, you will be very prosperous and happy all your life.\" Thus goes Lolo Hugo's story:","\"The mutya ng saging (magic charm from the banana plant) is considered the strongest and the most charming of all. Only the patient and the stout-hearted could find it.","The charm cannot be found in all banana plants. This plant grows apart from other banana plants. It produces no young suckling around it; not even weeds would grow within the shadow of Its leaves.","Look for that plant when it is about to bear flowers. You will notice that its purple heart is like that of any other banana plant. But it will open only at midnight when the moon is young. This banana tree will not bear fruit because the heart will fall off when the charm is gone. So, you will watch, night after night under this banana tree. At some time before midnight when the heart is about to open, the banana leaves will fly about as if tossed by strong winds, although there is not even the whisper of a wind animal shapes and shadows shall gather around the banana and frighten you away.","Stay beneath the heart and be ready to catch the stone as it falls out. If it falls to the ground, it will sink immediately to the center of the earth. When you catch the stone, swallow it fast because the weird creature will try to wrest it from you. They will bite your hands, tear your mouth. You must fight these creatures until you have swallowed the stone. If you succeed, the creatures, groaning and crying, will leave you alone and go back to where they came from.","Henceforth, love will be yours for the choosing. You will have many friends, and life will come easy and pleasant for as long as you live.\""],"q":[["Why did Lolo Hugo tell the children to listen very carefully to the story of the Mutya ng Saging?",["Because it was frightening","Because it was only for adults","Because the story was very short","Because it carried an important lesson for their future"],"D",null],["What does the difficulty of finding the Mutya ng Saging symbolize?",["Wealth is inherited","Fear should always be avoided","Luck comes easily to everyone","Success requires patience and courage"],"D",null],["Why do the frightening creatures appear when the banana heart is about to open?",["To entertain the seeker","To destroy the banana plant","To punish people who are curious","To guard the charm and test the seeker’s bravery"],"D",null],["What does swallowing the stone before the creatures take it suggest?",["Magic works only at night","Victory comes through strength alone","Rewards must be hidden from others","One must act quickly and decisively to succeed"],"D",null],["What is the main message of the story about happiness and prosperity?",["They are given freely to everyone","They depend on magical objects alone","They come only from supernatural beings","They are achieved through courage, patience, and perseverance"],"D",null]],"keyBy":"claude"},{"id":"L6-8-5","label":"Activity 5","title":"The Winning of Atalanta","focus":"Critical","p":["Atalanta, a beautiful and fearless maiden, was the daughter of Scheneus, King of Boetia. What she was most famous for was the fact that she had the fastest pair of feet in the whole world.","Because of her beauty she had many suitors, but proud Atalanta would not look with favor on any of them.","She said, \"I will marry only the man who is a faster runner than l.\"","She told her suitors that whoever really wished to marry her would have to run a race against her. If they failed, they would be put to death. Strangely enough, many of her suitors tried. They lost the race and thus lost their lives. Hippomenes, one of her suitors, watched every race and saw that he could never win against Atalanta. Only a miracle would help him, so he went to Venus, goddess of love and lovers for help.","Venus took pity on him and gave him three golden apples from her garden in the island of Cyprus. She whispered to him the secret of how to use the apples.","Happily, Hippomenes sought Atalanta and asked her permission to run a race with him. Atalanta looked at the young man and her heart filled with pity for him. She tried to persuade him not to try because he might lose his life.","\"I shall gladly die, for without you; life will not be worth living, “Hippomenes said. Finally, with great reluctance, Atalanta agreed to run a race with him.","\"Venus, don't forsake me now,\" he whispered as they took their places for the race.","Off they went. Hippomenes was fast but she gained on him and was about to overtake him when he threw one of the golden apples on her path. The girl was attracted by the glitter, so she paused one moment in her flight, scooped up the apple from the ground and ran on.","Hippomenes was quite a distance away, but fleet-footed Atalanta easily covered the gap between them. Soon he heard the swift patter of her feet close behind.","\"Venus, kind goddess, do not abandon me now, \"and he tossed the second apple at the feet of Atlanta. For the second time, Atalanta paused, scooped up the apple from the ground then ran on.","Hippomenes' lead did not do him much good for soon Atalanta was close again at his heels and was about to pass him.","The goal was near His heart beating with joy, Hippomenes threw third golden apple, and it rolled along Atalanta could not resist it. She paused only a brief second and ran again. But before she could catch up with Hippomenes, the young man, with one great bound, reached the goal and won the race.","They were married and lived happily and had many beautiful and brave descendants."],"q":[["Was Atalanta’s condition for marriage (racing suitors to death) justified?",["No, because running is unfair to men","Yes, because the suitors chose to accept the risk","Yes, because she had the right to choose any test she wanted","No, because the punishment was too extreme for losing a race"],"D",null],["How should Hippomenes’ use of the golden apples be judged?",["Wrong because Venus forced him to cheat","Fair because intelligence is also a form of strength","Completely fair because Atalanta agreed to the race","Unfair because it involved divine trickery rather than skill"],"B",null],["What does Atalanta’s repeated stopping for the apples suggest about her character?",["She was careless and foolish","She wanted Hippomenes to win","She was easily distracted and curious","She valued beauty and desire over her own rule"],"D",null],["Who bears the greatest responsibility for the deaths of the earlier suitors?",["Atalanta, for setting the rule","The suitors, for agreeing to race","Fate, which could not be avoided","Venus, for interfering in human affairs"],"A",null],["What critical lesson does the story suggest about power and pride?",["Love excuses all actions","Power should always be challenged","Pride can lead to unfair or cruel decisions","Strength is more important than wisdom"],"C",null]],"keyBy":"claude"},{"id":"L6-8-6","label":"Activity 6","title":"Our Neighbor, Taiwan","focus":"Critical","p":["North of the Philippines lies our closest neighbor. It is commonly known as the Republic of China (R.O.C.), or in most international organizations, as \"Chinese Taipei.\" In area, it is about the size of Mindanao. Taiwan was once a poor, underdeveloped country---enjoy a high standard of living and has a favorable balance of trade. have been put up so fast that new ones are housed under bamboo shelters while permanent buildings are under construction.","Farming has been made progressive and modern. Peasants wading in water-filled rice paddies behind water buffaloes have given way to prosperous farms with tractors and other machines. Bamboo huts have disappeared, replaced by brick stone houses. Taiwan has succeeded because of its people. The average Taiwanese is industrious and self-reliant and is a willing helper to his neighbor when someone needs help.","Taiwan has realized that if it were to prosper, being basically agricultural country, agriculture must be given primary attention. Instead of first thinking of steelmills;factories,jet, airports, giant buildings and magnificent homes as other underdeveloped countries do, Taiwan began with a program beginning at the grassroots --- improvement of farming and satisfying the people's hunger for land.","Taiwan instituted a well-organized land reform program with the aim of providing security and livelihood for the tenants of big landholders. It opened the public land for purchase by tenant-farmers, payable from their crop yield. Finally, large private landholdings were brought by/the governments and sold to the tenants at reasonable rates. No landlord could hold more than seven acres of Riceland. The former owners of large tracts of land were encouraged to invest in industrial and business enterprises.","As a result, Taiwan is self-sufficient in the production of country's immediate needs. They produce enough clothing materials. They have an excess of cement production and export over one million tons a year. They have medical and pharmaceutical plants, flour mills, motor cars, and truck factories, chemical fertilizer plants and many other enterprises. Educational and cultural development in Taiwan is also at a high level. About 600 billion New Taiwan dollars is spent on education. Taiwan's literacy rate as of 2003 is a high 96.1percent of its population."],"q":[["Why did Taiwan choose to prioritize agriculture instead of heavy industries at the start of its development?",["Because it lacked educated people","Because it wanted to imitate other countries","Because it had no interest in industrial progress","Because addressing basic needs was essential for long-term growth"],"D",null],["How did land reform contribute to Taiwan’s economic success?",["It made landlords richer","It reduced farming activities","It discouraged private investment","It gave farmers security and motivation to produce more"],"D",null],["What does the passage suggest about the role of people in Taiwan’s development?",["Natural resources guaranteed success","Government action alone caused progress","Development depended mainly on foreign aid","People’s attitudes and work ethic were key factors"],"D",null],["Which development approach in the passage is implied to be less effective for poor countries?",["Investing first in agriculture","Improving education and literacy","Helping tenant farmers own land","Starting with large factories and grand projects"],"D",null],["What lesson can other developing countries learn from the experience of Taiwan?",["Focus only on exports","Depend mainly on industrialization","Ignore cultural and educational development","Strengthen basic sectors and human development first"],"D",null]],"keyBy":"claude"},{"id":"L6-8-7","label":"Activity 7","title":"Africa, the Dark Continent","focus":"Critical","p":["Whenever we think of Africa, we seem to picture a large continent covered with either huge desert, high plateau and mountains, and vast impenetrable jungles inhabited by black uncivilized people. We oftentimes overlook the past that Egypt, which is part of Africa, was once cradle of the first great civilization. Egypt had built magnificent temples, palaces, and the pyramids when most on Europe was still in a barbaric stage. Africa has often been called the Dark Continent.","The true Africa is a continent of vivid contrasts. There are many kinds of landforms and the climate is variable. There are the hot desert Sahara Desert and the high plateau which covers the area south of the Sahara. There are dense jungles, broad fertile plains and high snow-capped mountains. As a result of these differences, many ways of living have developed in Africa's varied kinds of lands.","The areas north of the Sahara have been part of the rich and colorful life of the Mediterranean world. Sahara's great expanse is not entirely a waste of shifting sands. Only one-tenth of the desert is covered by sand. The rest are plains, hills, mountains, and thousands of oases where date palms and other palms thrive. Egypt and Ethiopia are regions which are productive, culturally and agriculturally. Theirs is a rich history. Both the eastern and western coastal areas are fertile. Here are cites of the cities and other centers of population. Southern Africa is a pleasant land and is one of the continent's most prosperous areas. Central Africa's rivers have their source here.","Central Africa is covered with dense jungles. So much rain falls that trees and plants grow very fast. Thick bushes and tough vines twinning from trees make passage difficult, sometimes impossible. The jungles are inhabited by pygmies who never mingle with other people.","They depend on fruits, roots, and small animals which they kill with poisoned arrows. Elsewhere, the plateaus are grasslands with only a few trees. The grasslands with all grass are the home of elephants, buffaloes, and rhinoceros. The areas where the grass is not very tall are the roving areas of all kinds of deer, giraffes, and zebras.","The African plateau is peopled by Negroes (Bantu)who have little contact with civilization. They are very tall men; men seven feet in height are common sight. Many of them earn their living through primitive farming, hording, and hunting, they have a tribal form of government; each village has a headman, usually related to the chief of the tribe. There are many war-like tribes in the heart of Central Africa.","The civilized people of Africa are mostly Muslims. The Muslim Arabs were the first people to have commercial and cultural contact with Africa. Christian missionaries have made inroads in the establishment of Christianity in many parts of Africa.","With these innovations, could we say then that Africa is no longer the Dark Continent?"],"q":[["Is the term “Dark Continent” fair?",["No","Yes","No, it is misleading","No, it ignores Africa’s history and diversity"],"D",null],["Why does the author mention ancient Egypt early in the passage?",["To praise Europe","To describe geography","To show Africa’s early greatness","To challenge the idea that Africa lacked civilization"],"D",null],["What weakness appears in the author’s descriptions of some African groups?",["Vagueness","Exaggeration","Cultural bias","Use of outdated and judgmental terms"],"D",null],["How should modern readers react to words like “uncivilized” and “primitive”?",["Accept them","Ignore them","Question the language","Analyze them as products of their time"],"D",null],["Based on the passage, what is the strongest reason Africa should no longer be called the “Dark Continent”?",["It has deserts","It has many tribes","It has fertile lands","It has diverse cultures, histories, and levels of development"],"D",null]],"keyBy":"claude"},{"id":"L6-8-8","label":"Activity 8","title":"Jose Rizal and Benigno Aquino Jr.","focus":"Creative","p":["It is often said that it is difficult to make comparisons of two people, even twins, who may have different idiosyncrasies, mannerisms, qualities, principles, and so forth. The opposite to this could be easy.","Benigno Aquino, or Ninoy for one, was likened to Jose Rizal in courage and service to the nation. Rizal had spent years in Europe studying and writing and had incurred the wrath of the friars with his novels Noli Me Tangere and El Filibusterismo.","Benigno Aquino had incurred the intense hatred of Ferdinand Marcos for his political activities including speeches on political platforms and in the Senate highly critical of the then Chief Executive. The friars ran after Rizal, but they could not get their hands on him as long as he stayed abroad. Obviously, when he announced that he was coming home, his friends and relatives hastened to tell him to stay abroad.","Ninoy was one or only the reason why Marcos declared Martial Law. He was immediately arrested by the military, tried before a military tribunal on charges of murder, possession of prohibited firearms and communism and was finally condemned to die before a firing squad. From the start, Ninoy refused to submit to trial, saying that the military judges' Commander- in- chief, who was Marcos, had already publicly condemned him on the same charges. He also refused to be represented by counsel.","\"Sirs,\" he addressed the tribunal, \"I know you to be honorable men. You may decide to preserve my life, but Marcos chooses to send me to death. Some people suggest that I beg for mercy. But this, in conscience, I cannot do. l would rather die on my feet with honor than live on bended knees with shame.\"","One day he suffered terrible chest pain and rushed to the Heart Center. The doctors told him that he needed a coronary bypass of which the Center is incapable of performing. The facilities were inadequate. Ninoy went to Houston, Texas, and received a triple bypass. Three years later, he wanted to come back but Malacañang did everything to keep him out. Marcos and Imelda told him the Communists would assassinate him if he came home. His family and friends also warned him of the danger. However, he managed to come home with a false passport, taking China Airlines. On arrival he was whisked out from his seat and assassinated while going down the stairs.","Rizal had likewise defied his family and friends and came home. He was arrested and sentenced to die by firing squad at the Luneta.","Rizal and Aquino will live in history with parallel deeds for their countries and similar claims to the gratitude of the Filipinos. Rizal's death incensed the revolution against Spain. Aquino's death sparked and ignited the peaceful and bloodless revolution that ousted Marcos from power and sent him out the country."],"q":[["If Jose Rizal and Benigno Aquino Jr. were alive today, how might they express their opposition to injustice?",["By remaining silent to stay safe","By avoiding politics completely","By working only in foreign countries","By using modern media and public platforms to speak out"],"D",null],["If Rizal had chosen not to return to the Philippines, how might Philippine history have changed?",["The Filipinos would have forgotten Rizal","Nothing important would have changed","Spain would have immediately granted independence","The revolution might have been delayed or taken a different course"],"D",null],["Imagine Ninoy Aquino chose to stay abroad permanently. What effect might this have had on the Filipino people?",["They would have become richer","Martial Law would have ended sooner","Marcos would have resigned immediately","They might not have been inspired to rise against dictatorship"],"D",null],["What modern leader could be compared to Rizal and Ninoy based on the qualities shown in the text?",["A leader who avoids criticism","A leader who seeks power at all costs","A leader who values wealth over principles","A leader who risks personal safety to serve the people"],"D",null],["If you were asked to write a new title for the essay, which would best capture its message?",["Two Men Who Lost Their Lives","The Power of Courage and Sacrifice","Political Conflicts in the Philippines","A Comparison of Two Leaders"],"B",null]],"by":"Alberto G. Romulo","keyBy":"claude"},{"id":"L6-8-9","label":"Activity 9","title":"The Sea Horse","focus":"Creative","p":["Nature has many strange creatures, some of them huge, some of them small. One of these strange creatures is the sea horse. This animal of the sea has the arching neck of a wild stallion, the swelling bosom of a pigeon, the grasping tail of a monkey, and the power to change its color that belongs to a chameleon. It has eyes that pivot independently of one another so that while one looks up, the other can look down or around. To top this fantasy, the male-is equipped with a kangaroo style pouch in its front, from which the little ones are born.","There are more than 40 species, ranging in length from one inch to one foot, and they can be found in the warm seas all over the world. The sea is a master of camouflaging. It can change color to go with its surroundings.","The sea horse's body is sheathed in a tough, Capiz-like armor of bony plates. Because of this rigid armor, it swims standing up by fluttering a tiny yellow, fanlike fin on its back, propelling itself in a stately manner. Straight as a rod, it moves forward or backward, up and down, by manipulating that fin so fast that movement is hardly visible. Two membrane- like fins at the sides of its head wave carelessly.","The worst thing that can happen to a swimming sea horse is a puncture in its armor. A single puncture in its armor could cause air to escape from its sealed armor and make the sea horse sink helplessly to the bottom. There it remains until he has manufactured enough gas to fill up his tank.","The courtship of sea horses is strange. The female produces from 500 to 600 eggs and deposits them in the male's pouch. For the next 45 days, the father sea horse nourishes the nestling in his swelling incubator pouch. At the end of this period, a baby sea horse the size of a comma swims out, followed by another, then another. This continues until the whole brood is ejected from the incubator. Each baby is an exact replica of its parents, except that the body is transparent. Its tiny heart can be seen beating with aid of magnifying glass.","The sea horse feeds on plankton and minute plant and animal life. It would not feed on anything that is not alive and moving. The sea horse itself is not fit to be eaten either by man or by fish.","What use is a sea horse? Studies made have not revealed any useful purpose in the sea horse. However, everything in nature has a need and a purpose. Perhaps, sea horses were made to keep the level of plankton down so that the latter would not overrun the sea."],"q":[["If the sea horse could speak, what do you think it would complain a bout most?",["Living only in warm seas","Being too small to be noticed","Being misunderstood by humans","Having armor that makes swimming difficult"],"D",null],["If the sea horse did not have the ability to change color, how might it survive?",["By swimming faster","By growing larger","By attacking other sea animals","By hiding among rocks and plants"],"D",null],["Which invention could be inspired by the sea horse’s body design?",["A fast racing boat","A flying machine","A land vehicle","A flexible underwater robot with armor"],"D",null],["If humans were like sea horses, what part of society would change t he most?",["Sports","Education","Transportation","Family roles and parenting"],"D",null],["If scientists discovered that sea horses suddenly disappeared, what might happen to the ocean?",["The water would dry up","Fish would become extinct","Coral reefs would turn into sand","Plankton might increase uncontrollably"],"D",null]],"keyBy":"claude"},{"id":"L6-8-10","label":"Activity 10","title":"Abraham Lincoln","focus":"Creative","p":["Many stories have been told of the boy who was born in a log cabin of very poor parents; of how he learned to read by the light of the fireplace; and of how he walked long distances to borrow books. From humble beginnings, he rose and, became the Great Emancipator.","As a young man, he tended store for a neighbor. On one occasion he unintentionally short-changed a customer. It was long after the customer had departed when he discovered his mistake. He walked miles and miles to the customer's house just to return the change. That was how he earned the name \"Honest Abe\".","Then he became President of the United States. When he was troubled by affairs of state, he took long walk to clear his mind. On these walks he was usually dressed in his old clothes. With his long strides he often outdistanced the men assigned to guard him.","At dusk of one winter, he went on one of these walks. Snow had covered the roads making them slippery. He was walking along an almost deserted street, when a carriage driving fast overtook him. Suddenly, a dog ran across the street frightening the horses. They shied, driving the carriage to a deep ditch, its rear wheel stuck in the deep muddy ditch. The horses were unable to move the carriage no matter how they tired. The colored driver and the footman kept urging the horses, but the carriage-wheel sank deeper. The passenger, apparently a very wealthy and important man because of his elegant and rich garb, keep shouting at his men, waving his arms in anger and frustration. He had an appointment to keep at the White House.","The tall, bearded pedestrian came to the scene, and at a glance saw what had happened. He approached the carriage on the side stuck in the ditch, put his shoulders to the wheel, and signaled the coachman to prod the horses. At first, the carriage would not move. With another mighty heave of the tall pedestrian, the carriage finally moved out of the ditch. The coachman leaped to his place, the passenger sank to his seat, and the carriage drove on to the White House.","The passenger was just on tome for his appointment. In the President's room, he sat, a little impatient. In his country, he was an important man. In this wild. country, he did not relish being kept waiting. At last, a door opened. A tall, bearded man entered, his clothes splattered with mud and snow. He gravely took the suddenly limp hand of the dumb- stricken visitor, then went around the desk and, mud and all, sat on the chair of the President of the United States.","Etched on an old-fashioned ink stand on which the tall man dipped a quill pen was the name: A. Lincoln!"],"q":[["If Abraham Lincoln lived in today’s world, which action would best show his honesty and humility?",["Avoiding public attention completely","Letting others take responsibility for his errors","Quietly correcting a mistake even if no one noticed","Posting his achievements on social media every day"],"C",null],["How might the wealthy passenger’s view of leadership have changed after meeting Abraham Lincoln?",["He would lose respect for government officials","He would realize that true leaders serve others humbly","He would believe leaders should appear powerful and rich","He would think leaders should never help ordinary people"],"B",null],["If Lincoln had chosen not to help the stranded carriage, what message would the story send instead?",["Power excuses selfishness","Leaders should protect their status","Kindness depends on social rank","Leadership has little to do with character"],"A",null],["Imagine the story were told from the passenger’s point of view. What feeling would likely dominate the ending?",["Anger","Fear","Indifference","Embarrassment and respect"],"D",null],["What modern-day situation best parallels Lincoln pushing the carriage out of the ditch?",["A leader giving a formal speech","A president avoiding public contact","A leader delegating all responsibilities","A leader personally helping during a disaster"],"D",null]],"keyBy":"claude"},{"id":"L6-8-11","label":"Post-test","title":"Story of Love and Time","focus":"Mixed","p":["A long time ago, there lived on an island different feelings such as happiness, sadness, love, knowledge, etc.","One day, they got to know that the island is going to sink soon. All my feelings got worried. The feelings living on the island decided to leave it with their pre-constructed boats. Love was the only one who decided not to leave the island. The day came when the island began to sink. All the feelings were leaving on their boats. Love asked Richness to take him in his grand boat but he said that he already filled his boat with gold and money so he could not take him.","Love requested Sadness to take him along but he said that he is very sad so he wants to be alone. Next, he called for help to Happiness but he was so happy and joyful that he did not listen to Love and passed in front of him without paying attention to him.","Love became very sad when no one was taking him in their boat. He heard a voice, “Hey dear friend! Come along with me.” Love came to a safe place in a boat. Love was so overjoyed that he forgot to ask who he was when he asked Knowledge who brought him. He further asked Knowledge if he knew him. Knowledge said, “It was Time who brought you here”. Love asked, “Why did Time help me?” Knowledge replied with great wisdom, “Only Time knows the value of Love.”"],"q":[["Where did the feelings live?",["Sea","Town","Island","A sinking island"],"D",null],["Who helped Love escape?",["Time","Knowledge","Sadness","Richness"],"A",null],["Why did Richness refuse to take Love on his boat?",["He disliked Love","He wanted to be alone","His boat was full","His boat was full of gold and money"],"D","Interpretive"],["What does Happiness passing Love without noticing suggest?",["Happiness is cruel","Happiness ignores others","Happiness can be self-absorbed","Happiness may overlook others while enjoying itself"],"D","Interpretive"],["Was Love right to stay behind when the island was sinking?",["No, it was foolish","Yes, it showed bravery","No, it showed carelessness","Yes, it showed selfless devotion"],"D","Critical"],["Which feeling showed the least responsibility in the story?",["Sadness","Richness","Happiness","Happiness because he ignored someone in need"],"D","Critical"],["Why is Time the one who saves Love, according to the story?",["Time is powerful","Time is always present","Time understands all feelings","Time recognizes Love’s true importance"],"D","Critical"],["If Love had left early, how would the story’s message change?",["Love is weak","Time is unnecessary","Feelings must act fast","Love would lose its deeper meaning"],"D","Creative"],["If the story were retold today, who might represent “Time”?",["A teacher","A parent","An experience","Life experiences that teach value"],"D","Creative"],["Which modern situation best reflects the lesson of the story?",["Ignoring friends","Saving money","Enjoying success","Realizing someone’s value too late"],"D","Creative"]],"keyBy":"claude"}],"9":[{"id":"L6-9-0","label":"Pre-test","title":"The Smallest Fish in the World","focus":"Mixed","p":["Before 1925, the \"tabios\" was thought to have been the smallest fish in the world. A full-grown \"tabios\" measured a fraction over eleven millimeters. The \"tabios\" is found in Lake Buhi, in the Bicol Region of Luzon.","In 1925, Dr. Albert Herre, an ichthyologist, was assigned by the American government to identify and describe Philippine fishes. From a collection of the Philippine National Museum, he found the prototypes of a still smaller type of fish. There were 75 of the tiny fish which he named Pandaka Pygmaea in a bottle. They were so small that the \"tabios\" appeared big by comparison.","When Dr. Herre went back to the United States, he sent a few specimens to the Museum.","Stanford Dr.","National Herre’s Science findings were doubted because this fish remained a rarity. No one had ever seen a live specimen of the fish that Dr. Herre claimed was the smallest fish in the world.","On February 28, 1951, Ricardo Esguerra, one of the marine biologists working in the saltwater experiments station of the Bureau of Fisheries in Malabon, Rizal, excitedly called up the Bureau’s main office and announced that he had found live specimens of the Pandaka. Inocencio Ronquillo, an expert of the Bureau, hurried to the experiment station and studied the tiny fish which had eluded scientists for many years. He measured and weighed them. To confirm his findings, Ronquillo wrote to the curator of Stanford University Museum of Natural Science and asked for some specimens of the fish that Dr. Herre had taken before.","Finally, Dr. Leonard Schulz, the museum’s representative, pronounced that they were one and the same type. Thus after 25 years, the Philippines could at least claim to have the world’s smallest fish. The Pandaka has now been found in various parts of the Philippines.","The smallest fish is known under the scientific name of Pandaka Pygmaea. Samples have been sent to the museums and aquariums of cities all over the world.","The Pandaka’s average length when full-grown is 9.66 millimeters or about the size of a grain of rice. It has a moderately elongated body that is wedge-shaped when viewed from above. Its head is large in proportion to its size. Its eyes protrude. It has two rows of teeth on either side of the jaw, the outer teeth being larger and more widely set than the inner row."],"q":[["Why was the discovery of Pandaka pygmaea in the Philippines in 1925 considered historically important?",["It showed that all tabios populations had gone extinct","It was then regarded as the smallest known fish in the world","It was the first marine fish ever described from the Philippines","It proved that all small fish in the Philippines were juveniles"],"A",null],["What was Dr. Albert Herre’s main role in the history of Pandaka pygmaea?",["He first described and named Pandaka pygmaea in 1925","He reclassified tabios as Pandaka pygmaea in 1951","He proved that Pandaka pygmaea lives only in deep ocean trenches","He led a campaign to ban fishing of all small fish in the Philippines"],"B",null],["How did Ricardo Esguerra contribute to the story of Pandaka pygmaea in 1951?",["He replaced Pandaka pygmaea with tabios in scientific classification","He officially declared Pandaka pygmaea extinct in the wild","He moved all remaining Pandaka pygmaea to foreign aquariums","He rediscovered Pandaka pygmaea in the Philippines after fears it had disappeared"],"A",null],["In historical discussions, why is the local fish known as tabios often mentioned alongside Pandaka pygmaea?",["Tabios and Pandaka pygmaea are the same species under different general","Tabios is the scientific name that replaced Pandaka pygmaea","Tabios was initially thought to be the true smallest fish, later replaced by Pandaka pygmaea","Tabios is a local small fish whose fame was overshadowed by Pandaka pygmaea as the \"smallest fish\""],"C",null],["Which statement best describes the typical habitat historically associated with Pandaka pygmaea in the Philippines?",["Clear, fast- flowing mountain streams","Shallow coastal or estuarine areas, often near mangroves","Deep, open- ocean pelagic zones","High- altitude crater lakes far from the sea"],"D",null],["From a biological perspective, why is the tiny adult size of Pandaka pygmaea scientifically intriguing?",["It demonstrates that only deep- sea fish can become very small","It challenges ideas about the lower size limits of vertebrate bodies","It proves that all small fish grow into large fish later","It shows that small fish cannot reproduce successfully"],"B",null],["Historically, how did the \"smallest fish in the world\" label affect public and scientific attention toward Pandaka pygmaea?",["It shifted all attention away from Philippine biodiversity","It led to the immediate banning of all fishing in its habitat","It turned Pandaka pygmaea into a globally cited example of extreme miniaturization","It caused scientists to ignore Pandaka pygmaea as unimportant"],"A",null],["What does the historical replacement of tabios in popular discussion by Pandaka pygmaea as the \"smallest fish\" mainly illustrate?",["That local fish names are always incorrect","That only foreign scientists can discover important species","That large fish are more important than small fish","That scientific records and media can reshape which species the public notices"],"C",null],["Why is it important for modern students in the Philippines to learn about the historical discovery and rediscovery of Pandaka pygmaea?",["It teaches only that foreign scientists are superior","It shows that biodiversity is only found in other countries","It highlights the value of both foreign and Filipino contributions to understanding local biodiversity","It proves that small fishes are useless for food"],"B",null],["What conservation lesson can be drawn from the story of Pandaka pygmaea’s small size and specialized habitat?",["Conservation is only needed in remote, untouched wilderness areas","Only large, commercially valuable fish deserve protection","Protecting mangroves and coastal wetlands can help safeguard even the smallest, most specialized species","Small fish are too numerous to be affected by habitat loss"],"A",null]]},{"id":"L6-9-1","label":"Activity 1","title":"Let There Be No Grudges","focus":"Literal","p":["This story is about a young boy who got into a fight with his elder brother. Enoch is his name, and his elder brother would always pick on him to the extent of making him angry and feeling bitter later. One morning they were it again. They fought hard and exchanged blows with each other. Somewhat outmatched because Enoch was wanting in size and height, he took quite a beating. His elder brother’s muscles were too much for him. His pride and ego suffered the most. The whole incident left him bitter towards his elder brother. As a matter of fact, he refused to talk or look at his brother. He was gritting his teeth with anger for not being able to put up square fight.","Bedtime came. Their mother wanted to pacify them and told them to make up as soon as possible.","Enoch was still grumbling, but his eyes were fixed towards the elder brother’s direction. Then the mother spoke to the younger one. “Don’t you think you should forgive your brother before you go to bed?” Remember the Bible says, “Don’t let the sun go down on your wrath.” The younger son looked toward his mother with perplexity. He thought for a few seconds and then said, “Mother, how can I keep the sun from going down?” The boy’s question revealed that he had no intention of getting rid of his grudge.","So, the mother explained to her younger son and made him understand what life would be like when people hold grudges against each other. It is true that there are people with an unforgiving attitude and true too, that we cannot change another person’s heart. She further explained that we cannot keep the sun from going down. But we can keep it from setting on your anger. And that means we must forgive."],"q":[["What is the name of the younger boy in the story?",["Elias","Ethan","Ezra","Enoch"],"D",null],["Why was Enoch outmatched in the fight?",["He was much taller","His brother avoided punches","He lacked size and height","He refused to fight back"],"C",null],["What did Enoch refuse to do after the fight?",["Eat dinner","Talk to or look at his brother","Go to bed","Help with chores"],"B",null],["What Bible quote did the mother reference?",["\"Don't let the sun go down on your wrath\"","\"Love your neighbor\"","\"Forgive seventy times seven\"","\"Turn the other cheek\""],"A",null],["What was Enoch's literal response to his mother's advice?",["\"I'll try tomorrow\"","\"Mother, how can I keep the sun from going down?\"","\"He started it!\"","\"I forgive him now\""],"B",null]]},{"id":"L6-9-2","label":"Activity 2","title":"The Value of Work","focus":"Literal","p":["Everything has value, no matter how insignificant or simple it is. At the mere mention of work, one immediately connects with the idea of toil and fatigue, which pertains to a slave who overdoes things in a difficult manner. Everything in this world involves work.","In what light should work be viewed? Anything that comes from something done is achieved by means of labor and work. It is of two-fold nature, either mental or physical. Both are indispensable to the well-being of human society. Our attention is directed towards physical labor.","Christianity teaches us to regard work as something sacred and honorable. Work is duty. You must not only work hard, but you must also love it wholeheartedly. Without it, order and cleanliness can never exist in the house. A state of affairs would be a mess and discomfort would prevail.","People who work and stay busy have no time for hanging around, gossiping, or being with bad company. Women who are fond of work are more virtuous and purer.","There is no dull moment in their lives, there is so much to gain when they are physically occupied.","Be proud, therefore, and love your work. Do it diligently and make it your best. Treat it as an inseparable friend. Whatever the nature of your work may be, do it with care and diligence. If it is a domestic chore, do not find fault and make critical remarks, but put your hand at everything. If your work is to be really done well, if it is to gain merit for yourself, perform it with a good intention. Yes, be patient, for without patience, no labor, toil, or work can be meritorious in the eyes of someone above.","These lines speak well of this: Swiftly time speeds on its way. See that thou use it well. Let each hour of every day be a tale of wisdom to tell."],"q":[["What immediate association does the passage make with the mention of \"work\"?",["Sacred duty","Value and simplicity","Toil and fatigue","Mental achievement"],"C",null],["According to the passage, work has what kind of nature?",["Only physical","One-fold","Only mental","Two-fold: mental or physical"],"D",null],["What does Christianity teach about work?",["It is sacred and honorable","It causes gossip","It is optional","It leads to discomfort"],"A",null],["What do people who work and stay busy avoid, per the passage?",["Hanging around, gossiping, or bad company","Dull moments","Cleanliness","Virtue"],"A",null],["What must accompany work to make it meritorious \"in the eyes of someone above\"?",["Good intention and patience","Overdoing things","Fault-finding","Criticism"],"A",null]]},{"id":"L6-9-3","label":"Activity 3","title":"Taj Mahal: an Immortal Monument to an Emperor’s Love","focus":"Interpretive","p":["In Agra, India, stands an immortal monument to an emperor’s love. It was built by Emperor Shah Jahan for his third wife, the beautiful Mumtaz Mahal. Mumtaz, the queen, was born in 1593.","He was a good emperor, and he ruled wisely. During his reign, magnificent buildings and palaces were built. He had a goldsmith build an elaborate throne of rare gems. It was a long, low couch with golden legs. A canopy, which rested on twelve emerald pillars, was over the throne. A tree of diamonds, rubies, emeralds, and pearls stood beside it.","Then, in 1631, Queen Mumtaz Mahal died in childbirth. The emperor was grief-stricken. He forbade music in his court, gave up wearing the colored imperial robes, and wore only white for mourning. A sepulcher was built for her. Twenty thousand workers labored 20 years to complete it. The best artisans, sculptors, designers, and carvers in India and nearby countries were employed under the most renowned architect of those days, Ustad Ahmad.","The monument was made of precious stones and valuable materials. The glorious white marble used comes from India; garnets and lapis lazuli come from Ceylon. All in all, more than 40 varieties of precious stones were used, including turquoise mined in Tibet.","On the north side of the great highway leading to the sepulcher, a tall gateway, 80 feet high, guarded the entrance. The gateway doors were made of silver and studded with semi-precious stones. No less beautiful are the surroundings of the sepulcher. In the waters of the famous reflecting pool, the image of the tall dome floats eternally in blue. Everything around the place is made to draw the eyes to the central figure of the immortal monument to an emperor’s love, the Taj Mahal."],"q":[["What can be inferred about Shah Jahan's leadership style from the description of his building projects and throne?",["He ruled without consulting artisans","He prioritized military conquests over arts","He valued opulence and skilled craftsmanship","He avoided using foreign materials"],"C",null],["Why might the emperor's choice of white robes after Mumtaz's death symbolize more than simple mourning?",["It honored the workers' marble labor","It matched the silver gateway colors","It showed his preference for plain fashion","It represented purity of his undying devotion"],"D",null],["What does the involvement of artisans from \"India and nearby countries\" suggest about the monument's purpose?",["To make it a universal symbol of grief and love","To limit it to local Indian traditions","To create the cheapest possible structure","To compete with European cathedrals"],"A",null],["How does the reflecting pool's design infer the Taj Mahal's intended emotional impact?",["It hides flaws in the marble construction","It evokes eternal beauty mirroring the love","It creates a sense of fleeting impermanence","It focuses attention on the highway gateway"],"B",null],["What broader inference can be drawn about Mumtaz Mahal's role in Shah Jahan's life from the monument's scale?",["She influenced only his architectural tastes","Her death had little effect on his reign","She was a minor consort among many","She was central to his personal and ruling identity"],"D",null]]},{"id":"L6-9-4","label":"Activity 4","title":"No Love, No Victory","focus":"Interpretive","p":["Love is an indispensable necessity for every human heart. But it is of great importance, especially for every young person, to have in his heart a true, genuine, and abiding love of God.","It is said that the decisive battles to be fought are with the devil, the world, and the flesh. However, with the power of love - the love of God - all these can be conquered. It is only when the ardent love of God dwells in one’s heart that he may hope to speak of victories. The history of the world, the pages of sacred history, and the history of each individual alike teach us that without love, there can be no victory.","Love, in a general sense, conquers both good and evil things. Think of what inspired heroes in all ages and led them to achieve immortal deeds of glory. What inflamed the heart of Napoleon the Great, pushing himself forward without rest and conquering countries of Europe? It was love for their fatherland. What causes the miser to suppress the strongest impulse of nature, the desire for food and drink, and literally to die of hunger beside his stock of gold? It is the love of money. What usually compels lovers to commit suicide, conquering even the love of life? Again, it is love which has been rejected.","What is the motive which makes a mother overcome her desire for ease and comfort, sacrificing money, time, health, and everything? It is love for her child. What enables good Christian married people to practice self-control, to overcome selfishness, and to set aside their own wishes? It is love and conjugal affection for each other. And how was the greatest victory over sin, death, and hell, the victory won by the Redeemer triumphant? It was a victory of the love of God for us, human beings. Such is the all-conquering might of love. Without it, there is no victory."],"q":[["What can be inferred as the primary source of power needed to win \"decisive battles\" against the devil, world, and flesh?",["Personal willpower and strategy alone","Ardent love of God dwelling in the heart","Historical knowledge from sacred pages","Rejection of all earthly affections"],"B",null],["From Napoleon's conquests and the miser's starvation, what does the passage imply about love's influence on human behavior?",["Love conquers natural impulses like rest, hunger, or self-preservation","Love only motivates positive, heroic deeds","Love weakens resolve in ambitious people","Love requires wealth to achieve victories"],"A",null],["What can be inferred about a mother's sacrifices from the passage's example?",["They stem from selfishness masked as care","They prioritize her own comfort over the child","They prove love conquers desire for ease, time, and health","They are less powerful than romantic love"],"C",null],["Based on the lovers' suicide and spouses' self-control, what universal truth about love emerges?",["Love always leads to harmony without struggle","Love compels overcoming even the instinct for life or selfishness","Love fades when rejected or challenged","Love succeeds only in marriage, not individually"],"B",null],["How does the passage imply Christ's victory over sin, death, and hell relates to all other examples?",["It demonstrates God's love as the greatest, all-conquering force","It shows victory without any personal cost","It relies on human effort more than divine love","It is unrelated to earthly loves like patriotism"],"A",null]]},{"id":"L6-9-5","label":"Activity 5","title":"What is Cadetship?","focus":"Critical","p":["There is more to the glamour and prestige that has always been associated with the Philippine Military Academy; that is, the sacrifice and truth. Blood, sweat, and tears are behind all of these.","First, it is blood, for it is the individual alone who can make himself survive the continuously fluctuating changes and pressures of Academy life. Second, is sweat - it is the painful process of changing from the carefree ways of civilian life to the exacting demands of military life. Third, are tears for the joy, pride, and feeling of fulfillment of having survived obstacles, something between life and death.","Let it be known, however, that nothing can be a much better source of pride than being able to conquer one’s weaknesses and turn them into strengths. Life begins with a very young man so eager to join the ranks of the “long gray line,” attracted by its glamour, only to be led down the wrong path upon entering and leaving sooner than he came.","The “Beast Barracks” or Summer Training is something that this same young man feels is too much for him. But it takes some attitude and courage, or guts, so to speak. Once he is in, he is exposed to things he has never dared to think of before. He does things he cannot imagine and wonders how they are done.","Anyway, it is every cadet’s goal as he prays to aspire above the level of common life and to adhere to the patterns of their creed - truth, nobility, and excellence.","The Summer Camp Training is a near-perfect program of development and training. One has to survive and surmount it through thick and thin. The feeling to quit is there after a rough time of one month or more.","But when Incorporation Day comes, every sacrifice is compensated. It is better that one finds out for oneself what it really is like to be a part of this closely-knit big family, that is, the Academy."],"q":[["According to the text, what does \"blood\" symbolize in the essence of cadetship?",["Individual survival amid pressures","Joy and pride from survival","Transition from civilian to military life","Feeling of fulfillment"],"A",null],["Why does the text describe \"Beast Barracks\" or Summer Training as pivotal for cadets?",["It guarantees Incorporation Day rewards","It exposes cadets to unimaginable challenges requiring guts","It eliminates all feelings of wanting to quit","It focuses only on academic excellence"],"B",null],["What is the cadet's ultimate goal as per the creed mentioned?",["Avoid all obstacles between life and death","Aspire above common life with truth, nobility, and excellence","Embrace carefree civilian ways","Conquer only physical weaknesses"],"B",null],["How does the text characterize the transformation during cadetship?",["Quick and painless shift to military demands","Tears only from joy on Incorporation Day","Painful sweat from civilian to exacting military life","Pride solely from initial glamour"],"C",null],["What compensates for the sacrifices of cadetship, per the text?",["Avoiding the urge to quit","Focus on weaknesses alone","Early exit from the Academy","Incorporation Day's joy, pride, and family bonds"],"D",null]]},{"id":"L6-9-6","label":"Activity 6","title":"Hannibal, the Great","focus":"Critical","p":["Carthage, a great city-state in North Africa, had grown into power. She had extensive colonies in the African continent, conquered Spain, and was in constant warfare with the Roman Empire and its satellite cities. The war, which had been going on between Rome and Carthage for nearly a century, had been indecisive, with the Carthaginians having lost and won as many battles as the Romans.","In Carthage, there arose a general, Hannibal, who almost destroyed the Roman Empire. No general had ever beaten the famed Roman army in as many battles as Hannibal had, and no army had caused so much fear and destruction as Hannibal’s army did.","Hannibal conceived of attacking the Romans in Italy. It was a feat never before made or even dared by any army. He began his invasion with 90,000-foot soldiers, 12,000 horsemen, and 40 elephants. He reached the foot of the Alps and crossed these mountains in nine days. Then, he met the Roman armies. By swift maneuvers, Hannibal managed to meet the Roman armies one by one. The sight of the giant elephants charging them brought terror to the Roman cavalry.","They fled from the fight and were easily defeated. On one occasion, Hannibal found himself completely surrounded. He ordered his army to fasten burning torches on the cattle horns. At midnight, he drove the animals toward a heavily defended enemy front. The terrible sight frightened the Roman ranks and destroyed them completely. In many of these battles, Hannibal’s forces were often outnumbered, but his elephants' juggernaut, the swiftness of his movements, and his brilliant command gave him victory.","For 16 years, Hannibal roamed the Italian peninsula, defeating all the armies sent against him.","Meanwhile, powerful Roman forces had been sent to Carthage. Hannibal was then recalled defending Carthage. However, his forces had been decimated in the Italian campaign.","At Zama, near Carthage, the powerful Roman army met Hannibal’s forces. Here, Hannibal met his defeat with a loss of 20,000 men. When peace was concluded, the Romans demanded Hannibal’s surrender to Rome.","To escape, Hannibal committed suicide."],"q":[["What was the primary strategic innovation of Hannibal's invasion that shocked the Romans?",["Naval blockade of Italy","Overland crossing of the Alps","Direct assault on Rome's walls","Alliance with Egyptian pharaohs"],"B",null],["At Cannae, Hannibal's double envelopment tactic succeeded because:",["Roman infantry charged predictably into his weak center","Elephants formed the front line","Night attack with torch-lit cattle","Superior Roman cavalry flanked him"],"A",null],["Why did Hannibal fail to capitalize on victories like Trebia and Trasimene for total Roman collapse?",["Lack of siege equipment and Italian city defections","Roman elephants outnumbered his","Immediate recall to Carthage","Gaul tribes betrayed him early"],"A",null],["At Zama, Scipio Africanus defeated Hannibal mainly by:",["Outnumbering his elephants 2-to-1","Neutralizing Carthaginian cavalry and luring elephants into lanes","Burning cattle horns at midnight","Starving the Carthaginian army"],"B",null],["Hannibal's suicide in Bithynia (Libyssa) exemplified his:",["Defiance against Roman pursuit despite political recovery in Carthage","Loyalty to Carthage over personal survival","Fear of capture after Zama victory","Alliance with Syrian king"],"A",null]]},{"id":"L6-9-7","label":"Activity 7","title":"A Good Turn Deserves Another","focus":"Critical","p":["In life, there are many things that happen which are least expected. When these things turn to blessings, people’s hearts are softened, and they look up above to praise Him in glorious words. Some may just leave occurrences and daily happenings to fate and may keep everything to themselves.","But have you ever gone out of your way to do something for someone and had it go unnoticed? Very often it is difficult to believe that doing good to others is worth the effort, especially when we don’t receive a “thank you” in return. And yet, serving without looking for a reward is just like walking with God peacefully. As Christians, we should not display a cash-and-carry attitude, expecting immediate appreciation for the good we do. We must remember that someday he will richly reward us.","A magazine article told of a businessman who facilitated his dealings in the car business with a very young foreigner. The latter bought a new car, and a very fast and honest transaction took place between them. Short of some thousand pesos to complete the full payment, the businessman agreed to collect the amount after two weeks. Two weeks passed, but the buyer still could not produce the amount as his money had not come yet. So a grace period was given and extended to another two weeks. Finally, the deal was over. Everything was paid for.","Ten years later, the young man had become the sole purchasing agent for the Saudi Arabian Contractor’s Association. To repay the businessman’s hospitality and to show his gratitude for all the conveniences afforded to him, he placed a million-dollar order for 500 heavy dump trucks and 200 pickups. The businessman blinked his eyes with disbelief. “It’s unbelievable!” he exclaimed. “I just can’t believe it, but it’s true.” The good he had done was rewarded years later, beyond his wildest imagination.","As the lines rightfully say: \"Be not weary in your serving; Do your best for those in need. Kindness will be rewarded, By the Lord who prompts the deed.\""],"q":[["What is the primary theological lesson the text draws from the businessman's story?",["God rewards only those who expect immediate payment for good deeds.","Patience in serving others leads to unexpected, abundant blessings over time.","Business success depends on trusting strangers without any risk.","Gratitude from others is unnecessary if faith is strong enough."],"B",null],["How does the text contrast a \"cash-and-carry attitude\" with Christian service?",["It portrays the attitude as more efficient for daily life than selfless acts.","It equates it to expecting instant rewards, unlike serving God without anticipation.","It suggests both approaches yield the same long-term results.","It recommends it for non-Christians but not believers."],"B",null],["The story of the young foreigner illustrates which key idea about rewards?",["Good deeds must be repaid within two weeks to be meaningful.","Grace periods in transactions guarantee future loyalty.","Business deals with foreigners always lead to profit.","Small acts of kindness can yield massive returns after a decade."],"D",null],["According to the text, why might people doubt the value of unnoticed good deeds?",["Due to a lack of verbal thanks, making the effort seem wasted.","Because blessings always come quickly and visibly.","Since fate handles all outcomes without human input.","As serving God requires public displays for validation."],"A",null],["What does the closing poem reinforce about the overall message?",["Rewards come only from human gratitude, not divine prompting.","One should serve only those who can reciprocate immediately.","Persistent kindness, even when tiring, is divinely rewarded.","Weariness in serving proves a lack of true faith."],"C",null]]},{"id":"L6-9-8","label":"Activity 8","title":"The Lion is Dying","focus":"Creative","p":["The lion is the largest and most celebrated member of the cat family. It has an apparently large-sized head because of the great mane which covers its head, neck, and shoulders. By the time the lion is five or six years old, it can weigh more than 500 lbs and can be over 9 ½ feet long from the tip of its nose to the tip of its tail.","The lion has the reputation of being fearless, but it is more accurate to describe it as sly. Its instincts are like those of a house cat, and it hunts like a cat. It quietly stalks its prey, then, making a short, quick bound, lands on it. It mauls and bites the head of its victim until its neck is broken, and its veins and arteries are torn open.","The lion will eat anything it can find. While in the prime of its strength, it will hunt all sizes of deer, zebras, and even buffaloes. However, as it grows older, it may live on rodents, scorpions, and tiny creatures, even rotting meat.","The lion was at one time known as the king of the jungle. The jungles of Africa, Asia Minor, and India were once the lion’s domain. But man has been slowly but surely exterminating the species. The lion has disappeared in Asia Minor, Iran, and even in many parts of Africa. The remnants of the lion family in Asia are now confined to Northwestern India’s GIR forest, a reservation. There, some 160 lions remain, a pathetic remnant of a once prolific species.","However, even in its sanctuary, the lions are losing their natural prey of deer and wild buffaloes because men have encroached on the sanctuary and have domesticated the buffaloes. The lions are forced to slaughter and feed on domesticated buffaloes, and the herdsmen retaliate by killing the lions.","The king of the jungle is fast becoming extinct. Unless man gives the lions a place where they can live and multiply peacefully, and where their natural diet of deer and other animals can also live, many may never see a lion again except in museums or in picture books."],"q":[["What does the passage imply about the lion's reputation for fearlessness?",["It stems from its massive size and mane alone","It reflects bold charges against large herds","It is overstated; the lion is actually sly and cat-like in instincts","It matches its habit of eating only prime prey like buffaloes"],"C",null],["How does the lion's diet as it ages interpret its adaptability?",["It shows preference for challenging hunts like deer","It remains focused on large prey throughout life","It declines to scavenging rodents and rotting meat out of necessity","It avoids small creatures to preserve strength"],"C",null],["What can be inferred from lions' confinement to India's GIR forest?",["Humans expanded jungles there for protection","The species thrives despite losing Asian Minor and African ranges","Man's extermination reduced it to a \"pathetic remnant\" of 160 lions","Natural migration led to this single sanctuary"],"B",null],["What does the sanctuary encroachment suggest about human-lion conflict?",["Lions gain more deer prey from domestication","Herdsmen support lions by providing buffaloes","Lions must raid livestock, prompting retaliation killings","Buffaloes multiply naturally inside the reserve"],"C",null],["The passage's title and conclusion interpret the lion's fate as?",["Thriving under man's protection in jungles","Evolving to new habitats like museums","Extinct unless given protected space with natural prey","Kingly due to past glory in Africa/India"],"A",null]]},{"id":"L6-9-9","label":"Activity 9","title":"It is Not \"Why?\" but \"What?\"","focus":"Creative","p":["When any misfortune or ill luck comes our way, we often ask, \"Why me, God? Why should this happen to me?\" Then we examine ourselves as to whether we are right in professing our faith or giving the appearance of being one who believes in the professed faith. Something dictates that perhaps it would be better if we ask, \"What can I learn from this? What is God teaching me?\"","This story is told by a missionary in China many, many years ago about a woman who boarded a train with him in Europe. The first thing the missionary noticed was her radiant face, so sweet, and troubles seemed far from existence. They talked about anything under the sun, and her face would brighten up when mission and religious work were discussed. Obviously, she had not experienced trying circumstances.","As the train sped off and their talk turned into intimate conversation, the missionary observed that the woman's right hand was missing, and in its place was a steel hook. As their talk continued, the missionary learned that this lady friend had been a missionary in India, had contracted a lung disease, and had been sent home to die. So, she returned to her native country, Sweden, bought a farm, and worked daily on it.","One day, while she was working on a threshing machine, her right hand was cut. It was a big blow, a great misfortune in her life, but she accepted it as one of life's trials. She continued: When my hand was cut off, I immediately looked up and said, \"Lord, what do you really want me to do now that my right hand is gone? What work? I was not asking why but what?\"","What a brave woman! She deserves all the praises this world possesses with her unflinching courage. Her strength of character is really commendable. God used her to turn her farm into a home for the aged, the sick, and the feeble. She is still there, bringing blessings to the unfortunate.","How many adverse circumstances come into your life? Our nature is to question God's designs and workings. He just wants you to show the genuineness of your faith and the adequacy of His grace. So stop asking why. God will show you what."],"q":[["What does the missionary initially infer about the woman's life from her radiant face?",["She had never faced trying circumstances","Her faith made troubles nonexistent","She avoided discussing personal hardships","Mission work erased all past pains"],"A",null],["The woman's response to losing her hand primarily illustrates which attitude toward misfortune?",["Resignation to life's inevitable trials","Immediate blame on external causes like machinery","Seeking God's purpose through \"What?\" instead of \"Why?\"","Focusing on physical recovery first"],"C",null],["How does the story interpret the role of adversity in testing faith?",["It exposes weaknesses in professed belief","God uses it to reveal grace's adequacy","Believers must endure without questioning","It proves the insincerity of religious work"],"B",null],["What transformation resulted from the woman's \"What?\" question after her injury?",["She abandoned farm work for full-time ministry","Her farm became a home blessing the unfortunate","She returned to missionary service in India","The threshing accident was deemed divine punishment"],"B",null],["The story's core lesson equates asking \"What?\" with demonstrating:",["Unflinching courage and strength of character","Superior faith over others' questioning nature","Freedom from all adverse life circumstances","Praise-worthy bravery alone"],"A",null]]},{"id":"L6-9-10","label":"Activity 10","title":"Aklan’s Most Illustrious Son","focus":"Creative","p":["Jaime L. Cardinal Sin was born on August 31, 1928, in New Washington, Aklan, to Juan Sin and Maxima Lachica. He was ordained as a priest on April 3, 1954; served as the first Rector of St. Pius X Seminary in Roxas City from 1957 to 1967; appointed Auxiliary Bishop of Jaro in March 1967 and later Archbishop of Manila on March 19, 1974. Finally, he was named a Cardinal at the Consistory on May 26, 1976. His chosen motto was “Serviam,” which means “to be a servant.” Cardinal Sin generously gave himself in complete service and absolute devotion to God and His flock. He displayed dynamic and militant leadership, which was his inspiration in his deep faith and commitment to lead his people to Jesus Christ.","During his elevation to the Cardinalate, he urged everyone to join him in a revolution, a revolution of love. Heeding the needs of his people, he tirelessly fought for the ideals of freedom, truth, and justice. He believed in the separation between the Church and State. Nevertheless, he asserted that the relationship between the two should be collaborative rather than adversarial.","Through his inspired leadership, he united the Filipino people to make the democratic process work in our country, and thus emerged as “People Power.” Cardinal Sin’s contagious sense of humor seemed to have become a byword. His wit and wisdom presented to us the partnership between humor and intellectual insight. He thought of himself as an elder brother in the task that the Lord had given him. Full of hope for a more humane citizenry, he turned to everyone on this land to tell them, “Let us choose the way of peace. And the way of peace is the way of justice and truth, the way of deep respect before God for the rights of each one.”","This was one of his favorite lines: “Man achieves the fullness of his humanity when his dignity is respected, when his freedom to do good is protected. Working hand in hand, the Church and the State can do so much to prevent man’s dehumanization.” Before his death in 2006, he had assailed the continuing graft and corruption in the government and had called for a more vigorous and unrelenting drive against these. His interest called for a change not only in the government but the private sector as well."],"q":[["What does Cardinal Sin's motto \"Serviam\" primarily reflect about his approach to leadership?",["Aggressive confrontation with political powers","Complete devotion as a servant to God and people","Strict separation from state affairs","Focus on personal humility alone"],"B",null],["During his elevation to Cardinal, what kind of \"revolution\" did Sin urge, and what does it signify about his vision?",["Violent overthrow of the government","A transformative shift toward love, freedom, truth, and justice","Economic reforms in Aklan","Internal Church restructuring"],"B",null],["How did Cardinal Sin view the ideal relationship between Church and State, despite believing in their separation?",["Completely independent and non-interacting","Adversarial to protect religious freedoms","Collaborative for the common good, like preventing dehumanization","State-dominant with Church submission"],"C",null],["What interpretive role did Sin play in fostering \"People Power,\" according to the biography?",["Military strategist against Marcos","Uniter of Filipinos to make democratic processes work through non-violent faith-inspired action","Silent observer of political events","Promoter of elite-only governance"],"B",null],["What broader change did Sin advocate before his death, beyond just government graft?",["Only military reforms","Anti-corruption drive extending to both government and private sectors for justice and peace","Focus solely on Church internal issues","Economic aid without accountability"],"B",null]]},{"id":"L6-9-11","label":"Post-test","title":"The Olympic Games","focus":"Mixed","p":["People all over the world are thrilled when athletic meets are held in various parts of the globe. World sports bring together athletes from all over. Do you know when and how these Olympic games came about?","The first Olympic games originated in Ancient Greece. These games were first held during the Panhellenic celebrations. Every four years, patriotic and religious games were held during the celebrations. The year 776 B.C. marked the first of such a recorded festival. The Greeks then began to keep their calendar by \"Olympiads\" from this date or by the four-year span between the games.","Simplicity marked the first Olympic games. It was said that the first consisted of only a foot race of about 200 yards on the plain near the small City of Olympia. As years passed, the kinds of games were increased and led to competitions that brought forward national pride and unity. The competitions were based on fair rules of sportsmanship and justice. However, at first, only Greek citizens were allowed to take part in the games. The winners were crowned with laurel and awarded wild olive and palm wreaths. Winners were also given many special privileges in their respective communities.","During the reign of the Roman rulers, these games went down to the level of carnivals and circuses. Theodosius, one of the Roman emperors, prohibited the holding of the games in 394 B.C. For almost 2,300 years, no Olympic games were held.","Then in 1894, a French educator, Baron Pierre de Coubertin, planned to revive the games. He invited many countries to participate in the athletic meets. The French Baron believed that by means of the youth’s love for sports, international understanding would develop.","In 1896, Baron Pierre de Coubertin was able to enlist nine nations to send their athletes to the first Modern Olympics. This marked the beginning of the participation of thousands of athletes representing nearly all nations to compete in these Olympic games which are held every four years in different countries. The Olympic Games are instrumental in bringing together athletes from.","Representatives of different countries compete with each other in games of skill and sportsmanship. The athletes get to know each other very well and learn that all races love the same ideals of fair play, courage, understanding, and friendliness. When the athletes return to their respective countries, they are better world citizens after having participated in activities replete with understanding and brotherhood."],"q":[["In what year was the first recorded Olympic festival held?",["394 B.C.","776 B.C.","1896","1894"],"B",null],["What marked the simplicity of the first Olympic games?",["Laurel wreaths for winners","Only a foot race of about 200 yards near Olympia","Participation by Roman citizens","Carnivals and circuses"],"B",null],["Who prohibited the Olympic games in 394 A.D.?",["Baron Pierre de Coubertin","Greek citizens","Theodosius, a Roman emperor","Panhellenic celebrants"],"C",null],["What can be inferred about the Greek calendar system from the text?",["It counted by months only","It used \"Olympiads\" as four-year spans starting from 776 B.C.","It began with Roman rulers","It ignored religious festivals"],"B",null],["Why were winners awarded special privileges in their communities?",["They represented international unity","Their victories promoted national pride during games","Games were held annually","Only non-Greeks competed later"],"B",null],["What led to nearly 2,300 years without Olympic games?",["Decline to carnivals under Romans, then prohibition","Increase in foot races only","Modern revival in 1894","Laurel wreath shortages"],"A",null],["The text critically suggests Roman influence degraded the games by turning them into what? (Critical)",["Patriotic unity events","Fair sportsmanship competitions","Carnivals and circuses","Religious Panhellenic festivals"],"C",null],["Baron de Coubertin's belief implies what interpretive goal for modern Olympics? (Interpretive)",["Limit to Greek citizens","Foster international understanding through youth sports","Crown winners with palms only","Hold games every year"],"B",null],["How does the text interpret the role of Olympic rules? (Interpretive)",["Based on injustice for unity","Promoted fair play, courage, and brotherhood among nations","Allowed only foot races","Excluded all privileges"],"B",null],["Critically, what makes athletes \"better world citizens\" per the text? (Critical)",["Winning laurels alone","Learning shared ideals like fair play from global interactions","Roman carnival participation","Local community privileges only"],"B",null]]}],"10":[{"id":"L6-10-0","label":"Pre-test","title":"The Statue of Liberty","focus":"Mixed","p":["More than a million people immigrate to the United States to start new lives every year, and if they are arriving in New York, one of the first sights that they will see is the Statue of Liberty. The Statue of Liberty stands on Liberty Island, near Manhattan in New York. Though she is often thought to be a resident of New York, Liberty Island is actually federal property, which means that the Statue of Liberty belongs to the whole country.","The Statue of Liberty is not only the tallest statue in America, but it is also one of the most recognizable American symbols. The Statue of Liberty is huge. From the tip of the torch to the pedestal on which she stands, she is just over 151 feet tall. If you include the pedestal in your measurement, she stands more than 305 feet off the ground. That’s more than 30 basketball hoops or an entire football field.","Her waist size is 35 feet, which would make it awfully tough to find pants, and the tablet she holds is 23 feet long. Don’t worry though; she hasn’t had any trouble holding that tablet yet with her 8-foot index finger. Talk about heavy-handed… Though America financed and built the pedestal on which the Statue of Liberty stands, the statue itself was a gift from France. In this way, the complete work, much like the United States, is a product of both American and French contributions.","At one time, America was ruled by the British. The founding fathers of America chose to fight against Great Britain for the independence of their country. France supported America by providing money, men, and weapons of war. Had it not been for French contributions during the Revolutionary War, America would not exist in the way that it does today; therefore, it is quite fitting that the Statue of Liberty, which represents freedom, came to being by a joint American and French effort.","On October 28th, 1886, just over one hundred years after America declared its independence from Great Britain, the Statue of Liberty was completed and dedicated by its designer, Frédéric Auguste Bartholdi. Perhaps no person did more to bring the statue into being than Bartholdi. Not only did Bartholdi gain both French and American approval for the project, but he also led the French fundraising efforts and designed the appearance of the statue.","The appearance of the Statue of Liberty is somewhat derived from Libertas, the Roman goddess of freedom. The torch represents how liberty enlightens the world. The seven points or rays coming from the top of the crown represent the sun, the seven seas, the seven continents, and (as with the torch) how liberty enlightens the world. Though much of the statue was modeled after depictions of goddesses, Bartholdi modeled the face after his mother.","Now that’s a Mother’s Day gift that’s hard to top. Though Bartholdi was responsible for the external appearance of the statue, the internal design can be largely credited to the innovative designer, Gustave Eiffel. Though Mr. Eiffel is best known for his contributions to a tower in Paris that is named after him, he also engineered the internal structure that holds up the Statue of Liberty. Eiffel chose to use a flexible structure so that changes in temperature and strong winds from the ocean would not cause the statue to crack.","Because of Eiffel’s crafty design, the Statue of Liberty may sway as much as three inches on a windy day. If the winds exceed 50 miles per hour, the torch may sway five inches. Eiffel is also responsible for including two spiraling staircases on the interior of the statue to help visitors reach the observation point in the crown.","While most people appreciate the Statue of Liberty today, during its construction in the 1870s, many Americans were critical of the project. Some took issue with the fact that Bartholdi was French. They believed that American monuments should be designed and constructed by Americans. Others felt that the statue wasn’t much of a gift since it required Americans to foot the bill for the pedestal. After the Panic of 1873, America fell into a deep economic depression that lasted through much of the 1870s. (During an economic depression, people spend less money and it is harder to find jobs.) Because the nation was going through a depression, many Americans thought that money should not be spent to support a giant French statue.","I think most Americans would now agree that it was a good investment."],"q":[["What is the height from the torch tip to the pedestal?",["Just over 151 feet","35 feet","23 feet","305 feet"],"A",null],["When was the Statue dedicated by its designer?",["1776","1873","October 28th, 1886","1906"],"C",null],["What does the waist/tablet size imply about clothing or holding?",["Easy to dress","Tough for pants, no issue holding tablet","Too small for visitors","Matches Eiffel Tower"],"B",null],["Why might Americans criticize the 'gift' during the 1870s?",["Bartholdi was American","U.S. paid pedestal amid depression","France built everything","No economic issues"],"B",null],["What enables the statue to sway in wind without cracking?",["Bartholdi's exterior","Eiffel's flexible internal structure","French concrete","Pedestal height"],"B",null],["What do the crown's seven rays critically represent?",["Seven U.S. states","French provinces","Sun, seas, continents, enlightenment","Bartholdi's family"],"C",null],["Critically, why fitting for French-U.S. joint effort?",["Britain funded it","French Revolutionary War aid for freedom","No historical ties","Eiffel disliked France"],"B",null],["Who credits for face model vs. internal staircases?",["Eiffel for face, Bartholdi for stairs","Bartholdi (mother's face), Eiffel (structure/stairs)","Both for everything","U.S. founders"],"B",null],["Interpret text's view: Good investment despite criticism?",["Yes, now iconic symbol","No, waste of money","Only for tourists","French regret"],"A",null],["In the late 19th century, some critics argued that funding the Statue of Liberty was a poor investment. From an interpretive, long-term perspective, which reason best explains why it can now be seen as a good investment despite that criticism?",["It generated immediate profit for all donors within a few years of completion","It replaced the need for any other public art or monuments in the United States","It became a powerful symbol that shaped national identity and democratic values over generations","It was primarily responsible for eliminating all immigration restrictions in the 20th century"],"C",null]]},{"id":"L6-10-1","label":"Activity 1","title":"Carver, a Man of Many Talents","focus":null,"p":["George Washington Carver was an African American. He was a research chemist with many remarkable achievements in chemistry, economics, and industry. George was born in 1864 to parents who had been slaves. The Civil War in America had set him free, but he chose to stay on the plantation of his former owner, Moses Carver. George learned how to care for plants instinctively and collected different plant species, creating his own botanical garden.","At the age of 8, George wanted to learn how to read, but the nearby schools were only for white children. He was given a blue-backed speller and learned all the words it contained in a short amount of time. Then, young George read the Bible. Perhaps his Bible readings helped him develop the ability to make friends.","At the age of 10, George left the Carvers to continue his studies. He found a home with the Watkins, who treated him like one of the family, similar to how the Carvers had done. George was a very fast learner and completed the entire primary school course in a single year. He worked his way through upper-grade school, high school, and college, ultimately earning a Bachelor of Science degree in Agriculture.","George was appointed head of the Department of Agricultural Research at a Negro university. There, he conducted an astonishing series of experiments. From peanuts, he developed nearly 300 products, including milk, butter, cheese, shaving lotion, breakfast food, flour, soap, ink, and cosmetics. From sweet potatoes, he developed 118 substances, including starch, vinegar, shoe blacking, library paste, and candies. He developed dyes from clay, onions, beans, and tomatoes. He made oil paints from clay, peanuts, and cattle manure.","From wood shavings, he created synthetic marble. However, peanuts were his main interest. Dr. Carver said, \"From peanuts, one can find food and shelter; he can also produce medicine, washing powders, face bleach, candy, wallboards for homes, and hundreds of other things.\""],"q":[["When was George Washington Carver born?",["1860","1864","1870","1880"],"B",null],["What did George learn to do instinctively as a child on the Carver plantation?",["Read books","Care for plants","Cook meals","Build gardens"],"B",null],["What book did young George read after mastering the blue-backed speller?",["A science textbook","The Bible","A history book","A dictionary"],"B",null],["From peanuts, how many products did George Washington Carver develop?",["Nearly 100","Nearly 200","Nearly 300","Nearly 400"],"C",null],["From sweet potatoes, how many substances did he develop?",["118","200","300","325"],"A",null]]},{"id":"L6-10-2","label":"Activity 2","title":"The Disappearance of Amelia Earhart","focus":null,"p":["One of the modern world’s intriguing sources of mystery has been airplanes vanishing in mid-flight. One of the more famous of these was the disappearance in 1937 of a pioneer woman aviator, Amelia Earhart.","On the second last stage of an attempted round the world flight, she had radioed her position as she and her navigator searched desperately for their destination, a tiny island in the Pacific. The plane never arrived at Howland Island. Did it crash and sink after running out of fuel? It had been a long haul from New Guinea, a twenty-hour flight covering some four thousand kilometers.","Did Earhart have enough fuel to set down on some other island on her radioed course? Or did she end up somewhere else altogether? One fanciful theory had her being captured by the Japanese in the Marshall Islands and later executed as an American spy; another had her living out her days under an assumed name as a housewife in New Jersey.","Seventy years after Earhart’s disappearance, ‘myth busters’ continue to search for her. She was the best-known American woman pilot in the world. People were tracking her flight with great interest when, suddenly, she vanished into thin air.","Aircraft had developed rapidly in sophistication after World War I, with the 1920s and 1930s marked by an aeronautical record-setting frenzy. Conquest of the air had become a global obsession. While Earhart was making headlines with her solo flights, other aviators like high-altitude pioneer Wiley Post and industrialist Howard Hughes were grabbing some glory of their own. But only Earhart, the reserved tomboy from Kansas who disappeared three weeks shy of her 40th birthday, still grips the public imagination. Her disappearance has been the subject of at least fifty books, countless magazine and newspaper articles, and TV documentaries. It is seen by journalists as the last great American mystery.","There are currently two main theories about Amelia Earhart’s fate. There were reports of distress calls from the Phoenix Islands made on Earhart’s radio frequency for days after she vanished. Some say the plane could have broadcast only if it were on land, not in the water.","The Coast Guard and later the Navy, believing the distress calls were real, adjusted their searches, and newspapers at the time reported Earhart and her navigator were marooned on an island. No one was able to trace the calls at the time, so whether Earhart was on land in the Phoenix Islands or there was a hoaxer in the Phoenix Islands using her radio remains a mystery.","Others dismiss the radio calls as bogus and insist Earhart and her navigator ditched in the water. An Earhart researcher, Elgen Long, claims that Earhart’s airplane ran out of gas within fifty-two miles of the island and is sitting somewhere in a 6,000-square-mile area, at a depth of 17,000 feet. At that depth, the fuselage would still be in shiny, pristine condition if ever anyone were able to locate it. It would not even be covered in a layer of silt.","Those who subscribe to this explanation claim that fuel calculations, radio calls, and other considerations all show that the plane plunged into the sea somewhere off Howland Island. Whatever the explanation, the prospect of finding the remains is unsettling to many. To recover skeletal remains or personal effects would be a grisly experience and an intrusion.","They want to know where Amelia Earhart is, but that’s as far as they would like to go. As one investigator has put it, “I’m convinced that the mystery is part of what keeps us interested. In part, we remember her because she’s our favorite missing person.”"],"q":[["In what year did Amelia Earhart disappear during her attempted round-theworld flight?",["1927","1937","1947","1957"],"B",null],["What was the destination island Earhart was searching for when she radioed her position before vanishing?",["Marshall Islands","Phoenix Islands","Howland Island","New Guinea"],"C",null],["Approximately how long was the flight from New Guinea to Howland Island, covering about 4,000 kilometers?",["10 hours","20 hours","30 hours","40 hours"],"B",null],["One fanciful theory suggested Earhart was captured by whom and executed as a spy?",["British forces","Japanese in the Marshall Islands","U.S. Navy","Coast Guard"],"B",null],["According to researcher Elgen Long, where is Earhart's plane likely located, within 52 miles of Howland Island?",["On land in the Phoenix Islands","Buried on a tiny Pacific island","In a 6,000-square-mile area of ocean at 17,000 feet depth","Captured in the Marshall Islands"],"C",null]]},{"id":"L6-10-3","label":"Activity 3","title":"Marie Curie","focus":null,"p":["Marie Curie was one of the most accomplished scientists in history. Together with her husband, Pierre, she discovered radium, an element widely used for treating cancer, and studied uranium and other radioactive substances. Pierre and Marie’s amicable collaboration later helped to unlock the secrets of the atom.","Marie was born in 1867 in Warsaw, Poland, where her father was a professor of physics. At an early age, she displayed a brilliant mind and a blithe personality. Her great exuberance for learning prompted her to continue her studies after high school. She became disgruntled, however, when she learned that the university in Warsaw was closed to women. Determined to receive a higher education, she defiantly left Poland and in 1891 entered the Sorbonne, a French university, where she earned her master’s degree and doctorate in physics.","Marie was fortunate to have studied at the Sorbonne with some of the greatest scientists of her day, one of whom was Pierre Curie. Marie and Pierre were married in 1895 and spent many productive years working together in the physics laboratory. A short time after they discovered radium, Pierre was killed by a horse-drawn wagon in 1906. Marie was stunned by this horrible misfortune and endured heartbreaking anguish. Despondently, she recalled their close relationship and the joy that they had shared in scientific research.","The fact that she had two young daughters to raise by herself greatly increased her distress. Curie’s feeling of desolation finally began to fade when she was asked to succeed her husband as a physics professor at the Sorbonne. She was the first woman to be given a professorship at the world-famous university. In 1911, she received the Nobel Prize in chemistry for isolating radium. Although Marie Curie eventually suffered a fatal illness from her long exposure to radium, she never became disillusioned about her work.","Regardless of the consequences, she had dedicated herself to science and to revealing the mysteries of the physical world."],"q":[["What can be inferred about Marie Curie's personality from her decision to leave Poland for education?",["She was easily discouraged by obstacles.","She prioritized personal comfort over ambition.","She was determined and defiant in pursuing goals.","She preferred informal learning at home."],"C",null],["Based on the description of her collaboration with Pierre, what inference can be made about their relationship?",["It was primarily competitive and independent.","It was harmonious and mutually supportive.","It focused solely on individual achievements.","It ended amicably before Pierre's death."],"B",null],["What does Marie's emotional response to Pierre's death and her subsequent actions imply about her resilience?",["She quickly recovered and abandoned science.","Grief intensified her distress but she overcame it through work.","She became permanently despondent and isolated.","Raising daughters eliminated her career ambitions."],"B",null],["From her acceptance of the Sorbonne professorship and Nobel Prize, what can be inferred about her dedication?",["She viewed awards as secondary to family life.","Barriers like gender did not deter her professional ascent.","She retired early due to health concerns.","Her success was accidental rather than earned."],"B",null],["Despite radium's fatal effects, what inference arises about Marie's commitment to science?",["She grew disillusioned and regretted her research.","She accepted risks for advancing knowledge.","She stopped experimenting after health warnings.","Personal safety outweighed scientific discovery."],"B",null]]},{"id":"L6-10-4","label":"Activity 4","title":"Mount Vesuvius","focus":null,"p":["Mount Vesuvius, a volcano located between the ancient Italian cities of Pompeii and Herculaneum, has received much attention because of its frequent and destructive eruptions. The most famous of these eruptions occurred in A.D. 79.","The volcano had been inactive for centuries. There was little warning of the coming eruption, although one account unearthed by archaeologists says that a hard rain and a strong wind had disturbed the celestial calm during the preceding night. Early the next morning, the volcano poured a huge river of molten rock down upon Herculaneum, completely burying the city and filling the harbor with coagulated lava.","Meanwhile, on the other side of the mountain, cinders, stones, and ash rained down on Pompeii. Sparks from the burning ash ignited the combustible rooftops quickly. Large portions of the city were destroyed in the conflagration. Fire, however, was not the only cause of destruction. Poisonous sulfuric gases saturated the air. These heavy gases were not buoyant in the atmosphere and therefore sank toward the earth and suffocated people.","Over the years, excavations of Pompeii and Herculaneum have revealed a great deal about the behavior of the volcano. By analyzing data, much as a zoologist dissects an animal specimen, scientists have concluded that the eruption changed large portions of the area’s geography. For instance, it turned the Sarno River from its course and raised the level of the beach along the Bay of Naples. Meteorologists studying these events have also concluded that Vesuvius caused a huge tidal wave that affected the world’s climate.","In addition to making these investigations, archaeologists have been able to study the skeletons of victims by using distilled water to wash away the volcanic ash. By strengthening the brittle bones with acrylic paint, scientists have been able to examine the skeletons and draw conclusions about the diet and habits of the residents. Finally, the excavations at both Pompeii and Herculaneum have yielded many examples of classical art, such as jewelry made of bronze, which is an alloy of copper and tin. The eruption of Mount Vesuvius and its tragic consequences have provided everyone with a wealth of data about the effects that volcanoes can have on the surrounding area. Today, volcanologists can locate and predict eruptions, saving lives and preventing the destruction of other cities and cultures."],"q":[["What can be inferred about the initial warning signs before the A.D. 79 eruption?",["Earthquakes provided clear months of advance notice.","Dormancy led residents to ignore subtle natural disturbances.","Roman authorities issued evacuation orders promptly.","The volcano showed glowing activity nightly."],"B",null],["Why was Herculaneum buried more rapidly than Pompeii?",["It received heavier rain of cinders and ash","Proximity allowed molten rock to flow directly into it first.","Fires spread faster on its rooftops.","Sulfuric gases targeted it exclusively."],"B",null],["What inference explains deaths in Pompeii beyond fire damage?",["All victims drowned in the Sarno River's flood.","Heavy poisonous gases sank and suffocated people.","Tidal waves swept through the streets.","Earthquakes collapsed all buildings instantly."],"B",null],["How do excavation methods imply skeletons were fragile?",["They dissolved in volcanic ash without treatment.","Washing with distilled water and acrylic paint preserved them for study.","Bronze jewelry protected them naturally.","Tidal wave debris embedded them deeply."],"B",null],["What broader inference about volcanoes stems from Vesuvius data?",["Eruptions never alter geography or climate.","Modern predictions save lives based on past events.","Artifacts alone predict future activity.","All effects are limited to local fires."],"B",null]]},{"id":"L6-10-5","label":"Activity 5","title":"The Battle of Marathon","focus":null,"p":["The victory of the small Greek democracy of Athens over the mighty Persian Empire in 490 B.C. is one of the most famous events in history. Darius, the king of the Persian Empire, was furious because Athens had interceded for the other Greek city-states in revolt against Persian domination. In anger, the king sent an enormous army to defeat Athens, thinking it would take drastic steps to pacify the rebellious part of the empire.","Persia was ruled by one man. In Athens, however, all citizens helped to rule. Ennobled by this participation, Athenians were prepared to die for their city-state. Perhaps this was the secret of the remarkable victory at Marathon, which freed them from Persian rule. On their way to Marathon, the Persians tried to fool some Greek city-states by claiming to have come in peace. The frightened citizens of Delos refused to believe this. Not wanting to abet the conquest of Greece, they fled from their city and did not return until the Persians had left. They were wise, for the Persians next conquered the city of Eritrea and captured its people.","Tiny Athens stood alone against Persia. The Athenian people went to their sanctuaries. There, they prayed for deliverance and asked their gods to expedite their victory. The Athenians refurbished their weapons and moved to the plain of Marathon, where their little band would meet the Persians. At the last moment, soldiers from Plataea reinforced the Athenian troops.","The Athenian army attacked, and Greek citizens fought bravely. The power of the mighty Persians was offset by the love that the Athenians had for their city. Athenians defeated the Persians in both archery and hand combat. Greek soldiers seized Persian ships and burned them, and the Persians fled in terror. Herodotus, a famous historian, reports that 6,400 Persians died, compared to only 192 Athenians."],"q":[["What primarily provoked King Darius to send an army against Athens?",["Athens' direct conquest of Persian territories","Athens' support for Greek city-states revolting against Persia","A trade dispute over resources at Marathon","Personal rivalry between Darius and Athenian leaders"],"B",null],["How did the citizens of Delos respond to the Persians' claim of peaceful intentions?",["They welcomed and allied with the Persians","They refused to believe it and fled their city","They joined the Athenians in battle preparations","They negotiated a truce independently"],"B",null],["What reinforcements arrived to support Athens just before the battle?",["Soldiers from Sparta","Troops from Delos","Forces from Plataea","Naval aid from Eretria"],"C",null],["According to Herodotus, what were the reported casualties in the battle?",["6,400 Athenians and 192 Persians","192 Plataeans and 6,400 Delians","6,400 Persians and 192 Athenians","Equal losses on both sides"],"C",null],["What factor does the account suggest offset Persian numerical superiority?",["Advanced Greek weaponry","Athenians' love for their city-state and citizen rule","Persian ships being burned early","Divine intervention alone"],"B",null]]},{"id":"L6-10-6","label":"Activity 6","title":"The Mystery of Anastasia","focus":null,"p":["One of the most intriguing stories of the Russian Revolution concerns the identity of Anastasia, the youngest daughter of Czar Nicholas II. During his reign over Russia, the czar had planned to revoke many of the harsh laws established by previous czars. Some workers and peasants, however, clamored for more rapid social reform. In 1918, a group of these people known as Bolsheviks overthrew the government. On July 17 or 18, they murdered the czar and what was thought to be his entire family.","Although witnesses vouched that all the members of the czar’s family had been executed, there were rumors suggesting that Anastasia had survived. Over the years, a number of women claimed to be Grand Duchess Anastasia. Perhaps the most famous claimant was Anastasia Tschaikovsky, who was also known as Anna Anderson. In 1920, 18 months after the czar’s execution, this terrified young woman was rescued from drowning in a Berlin river. She spent two years in a hospital, where she attempted to reclaim her health and shattered mind.","The doctors and nurses thought that she resembled Anastasia and questioned her about her background. She disclaimed any connection with the czar’s family. Eight years later, however, she claimed that she was Anastasia. She said that she had been rescued by two Russian soldiers after the czar and the rest of her family had been killed. Two brothers named Tschaikovsky had carried her into Romania. She had married one of the brothers, who had taken her to Berlin and left her there, penniless and without a vocation.","Unable to invoke the aid of her mother’s family in Germany, she had tried to drown herself. During the next few years, scores of the czar’s relatives, ex-servants, and acquaintances interviewed her. Many of these people said that her looks and mannerisms were evocative of the Anastasia that they had known. Her grandmother and other relatives denied that she was the real Anastasia, however.","Tired of being accused of fraud, Anastasia immigrated to the United States in 1928 and took the name Anna Anderson. She still wished to prove that she was Anastasia, though, and returned to Germany in 1933 to bring suit against her mother’s family. There she declaimed to the court, asserting that she was indeed Anastasia and deserved her inheritance. In 1957, the court decided that it could neither confirm nor deny Anastasia’s identity. Although it will probably never be known whether this woman was the Grand Duchess Anastasia, her search to establish her identity has been the subject of numerous books, plays, and movies."],"q":[["What year did the woman later known as Anna Anderson first claim to be Anastasia?",["1920","1922","1928","1933"],"C",null],["According to her story, how did Anna Anderson say she survived the execution of the czar's family?",["She hid in the basement during the shooting.","She was rescued by two Russian soldiers named Tschaikovsky.","She escaped with her grandmother's help in Germany.","She was smuggled out by Bolshevik sympathizers."],"B",null],["What was the outcome of the 1957 court decision regarding Anna Anderson's identity?",["The court confirmed she was Anastasia.","The court ruled she was an impostor named Franziska Schanzkowska.","The court could neither confirm nor deny her identity.","The case was dismissed due to lack of evidence."],"C",null],["Why did many of the czar's relatives, ex-servants, and acquaintances find Anna Anderson convincing at first?",["She provided detailed financial records from the Romanov fortune.","Her looks and mannerisms resembled the Anastasia they knew.","She passed multiple DNA tests linking her to the family.","She spoke fluent Russian and recalled state secrets."],"B",null],["What action did Anna Anderson take in 1933 after immigrating to the United States?",["She published a book detailing her escape.","She returned to Germany to sue her mother's family for inheritance.","She underwent DNA testing to prove her identity.","She renounced her claim and lived quietly."],"B",null]]},{"id":"L6-10-7","label":"Activity 7","title":"The First Flight","focus":null,"p":["Many great inventions are initially greeted with ridicule and disbelief. The invention of the airplane was no exception. Although many people who heard about the first powered flight on December 17, 1903, were excited and impressed, others reacted with peals of laughter. The idea of flying an aircraft was repulsive to some people. Such people called Wilbur and Orville Wright, the inventors of the first flying machine, impulsive fools. Negative reactions, however, did not stop the Wrights.","Impelled by their desire to succeed, they continued their experiments in aviation. Orville and Wilbur Wright had always had a compelling interest in aeronautics and mechanics. As young boys, they earned money by making and selling kites and mechanical toys. Later, they designed a newspaper-folding machine, built a printing press, and operated a bicyclerepair shop. In 1896, when they read about the death of Otto Lilienthal, the brothers’ interest in flight grew into a compulsion.","Lilienthal, a pioneer in hang-gliding, had controlled his gliders by shifting his body in the desired direction. This idea was repellent to the Wright brothers, however, and they searched for more efficient methods to control the balance of airborne vehicles. In 1900 and 1901, the Wrights tested numerous gliders and developed control techniques. The brothers’ inability to obtain enough lift power for the gliders almost led them to abandon their efforts.","After further study, the Wright brothers concluded that the published tables of air pressure on curved surfaces must be wrong. They set up a wind tunnel and began a series of experiments with model wings. Because of their efforts, the old tables were repealed in time and replaced by the first reliable figures for air pressure on curved surfaces. This work, in turn, made it possible for the brothers to design a machine that would fly.","In 1903, the Wrights built their first airplane, which cost less than $1,000. They even designed and built their own source of propulsion—a lightweight gasoline engine. When they started the engine on December 17, the airplane pulsated wildly before taking off. The plane managed to stay aloft for 12 seconds, however, and it flew 120 feet. By 1905, the Wrights had perfected the first airplane that could turn, circle, and remain airborne for half an hour at a time.","Others had flown in balloons and hang gliders, but the Wright brothers were the first to build a full-size machine that could fly under its own power. As the contributors to one of the most outstanding engineering achievements in history, the Wright brothers are accurately called the fathers of aviation."],"q":[["What does the passage imply about why some people ridiculed the Wright brothers' airplane?",["The high cost of the invention","Fear of the repulsive idea of machine-powered flight","Jealousy over the brothers' mechanical skills","Disbelief in their bicycle business success"],"B",null],["How did the Wright brothers' background in kites, printing, and bicycles most directly contribute to their aviation success?",["Provided funds for wind tunnel construction","Built practical skills in mechanics and aeronautics","Inspired them to copy Otto Lilienthal's designs","Led to immediate glider lift achievements"],"B",null],["Why did the Wright brothers reject Lilienthal's body-shifting control for gliders?",["It was too physically demanding","It required curved surfaces","Lilienthal's death proved it unsafe","They sought more efficient balance methods"],"D",null],["What was the primary outcome of the Wrights' wind tunnel experiments?",["Replaced inaccurate tables with reliable data","Confirmed existing air pressure tables","Developed a lightweight gasoline engine","Achieved lift without curved surfaces"],"A",null],["Which statement best evaluates the Wrights' progression from 1903 to 1905?",["Early flights lasted half an hour immediately","Abandoned gliders after initial failures","Perfected control for turns, circles, and 30-minute flights","Relied on balloons for sustained power"],"C",null]]},{"id":"L6-10-8","label":"Activity 8","title":"Magellan’s Voyage","focus":null,"p":["In the 16th century, an age of great marine and terrestrial exploration, Ferdinand Magellan led the first expedition to sail around the world. As a young Portuguese noble, he served the king of Portugal, but he became involved in the quagmire of political intrigue at court and lost the king’s favor. After he was dismissed from service by the king of Portugal, he offered to serve the future Emperor Charles V of Spain.","A papal decree of 1493 had assigned all land in the New World west of 50 degrees W longitude to Spain and all the land east of that line to Portugal. Magellan offered to prove that the East Indies fell under Spanish authority. On September 20, 1519, Magellan set sail from Spain with five ships. More than a year later, one of these ships was exploring the topography of South America in search of a water route across the continent. This ship sank, but the remaining four ships searched along the southern peninsula of South America. Finally, they found the passage they sought near 50 degrees S latitude. Magellan named this passage the Strait of All Saints, but today it is known as the Strait of Magellan.","One ship deserted while in this passage and returned to Spain, so fewer sailors were privileged to gaze at that first panorama of the Pacific Ocean. Those who remained crossed the meridian now known as the International Date Line in the early spring of 1521 after 98 days on the Pacific Ocean.","During those long days at sea, many of Magellan’s men died of starvation and disease. Later, Magellan became involved in an insular conflict in the Philippines and was killed in a tribal battle. Only one ship and 17 sailors under the command of the Basque navigator Elcano survived to complete the westward journey to Spain and thus prove once and for all that the world is round, with no precipice at the edge."],"q":[["What does the passage imply about the role of political intrigue in Magellan's career shift?",["It strengthened his loyalty to Portugal.","It forced him to seek opportunities elsewhere.","It made him abandon noble status.","It directly caused the papal decree."],"B",null],["How does the passage interpret the significance of the papal decree in motivating Magellan's voyage?",["It assigned all global lands equally.","It focused only on South America.","It prohibited Spanish exploration.","It provided a legal basis for claiming the East Indies."],"D",null],["What interpretive insight does the passage offer about the challenges of Pacific crossing?",["It was swift and well-supplied.","It highlighted human endurance limits through starvation.","Crews enjoyed panoramic views daily.","No ships were lost in the ocean."],"B",null],["The passage portrays Magellan's death as what kind of turning point?",["An avoidable delay in return.","A triumphant conquest","Proof of peaceful diplomacy.","Completion of the full circle."],"A",null],["What broader interpretation does the passage make about the expedition's legacy despite losses?",["It confirmed flat Earth theories.","Survivors empirically proved Earth's sphericity.","All ships returned intact.","Exploration avoided all risks."],"B",null]],"keyNote":"The module key skips item 2; items re-aligned to the questions."},{"id":"L6-10-9","label":"Activity 9","title":"The Spanish Armada","focus":null,"p":["Conflict had existed between Spain and England since the 1570s. England wanted a share of the wealth that Spain had been taking from the lands it had claimed in the Americas.","Elizabeth I, Queen of England, encouraged her staunch admiral of the navy, Sir Francis Drake, to raid Spanish ships and towns. Though these raids were on a small scale, Drake achieved dramatic success, adding gold and silver to England’s treasury and diminishing Spain’s supremacy.","Religious differences also caused conflict between the two countries. Whereas Spain was Roman Catholic, most of England had become Protestant. King Philip II of Spain wanted to claim the throne and make England a Catholic country again. To satisfy his ambition and also to retaliate against England’s theft of his gold and silver, King Philip began to build his fleet of warships, the Spanish Armada, in January 1586.","Philip intended his fleet to be indestructible. In addition to building new warships, he marshaled 130 sailing vessels of all types and recruited more than 19,000 robust soldiers and 8,000 sailors. Although some of his ships lacked guns and others lacked ammunition, Philip was convinced that his Armada could withstand any battle with England.","The martial Armada set sail from Lisbon, Portugal, on May 9, 1588, but bad weather forced it back to port. The voyage resumed on July 22 after the weather became more stable.","The Spanish fleet met the smaller, faster, and more maneuverable English ships in battle off the coast of Plymouth, England, first on July 31 and again on August 2. The two battles left Spain vulnerable, having lost several ships and with its ammunition depleted. On August 7, while the Armada lay at anchor on the French side of the Strait of Dover, England sent eight burning ships into the midst of the Spanish fleet to set it on fire. Blocked on one side, the Spanish ships could only drift away, their crews in panic and disorder. Before the Armada could regroup, the English attacked again on August 8.","Although the Spaniards made a valiant effort to fight back, the fleet suffered extensive damage. During the eight hours of battle, the Armada drifted perilously close to the rocky coastline. At the moment when it seemed that the Spanish ships would be driven onto the English shore, the wind shifted, and the Armada drifted out into the North Sea. The Spaniards recognized the superiority of the English fleet and returned home, defeated."],"q":[["What does the passage primarily imply about the economic roots of the Spain-England conflict?",["England's raids were minor and ineffective.","Spain's American wealth fueled English aggression and raids.","Philip II shared treasures voluntarily.","Drake's success depleted England's treasury."],"B",null],["How does the text interpret King Philip II's dual ambitions in launching the Armada?",["Purely economic retaliation against raids.","Defense of Portuguese ports only.","Religious restoration of Catholicism and throne claim in England.","Alliance-building with Protestant England."],"B",null],["What interpretive role does weather play in the Armada's early setbacks according to the passage?",["It completely prevented the fleet from sailing.","It forced a delay but allowed resumption, highlighting Philip's overconfidence.","English ships used it to raid Lisbon.","It aided Spanish regrouping in the North Sea."],"B",null],["The passage interprets the English burning ships tactic as what kind of strategic advantage?",["A direct assault on Spanish soldiers.","Signaling alliance with France.","Replenishing Spanish ammunition.","Creating panic and disorder, blocking maneuvers in the Strait of Dover"],"B",null],["Overall, what does the narrative suggest about the Armada's defeat beyond numerical superiority?",["English ships were larger and slower.","Spanish flaws like poor armament, weather shifts, and English maneuverability led to recognition of inferiority.","Philip's fleet remained indestructible.","The wind shift doomed England instead."],"B",null]]},{"id":"L6-10-10","label":"Activity 10","title":"Submarines and Submersibles","focus":null,"p":["Do you know the difference between a submarine and a submersible? A submarine is a watercraft capable of independent operation under the sea. Submarines do not require support ships because they can renew their air and power supplies independently. Submersibles also submerge and operate underwater, but they need the support of a larger vessel. Submersibles cannot renew their air and power supplies without support. For this reason, submersibles are usually smaller and cannot spend as much time underwater as submarines.","The first documented submersible was constructed in 1620 by Cornelius Drebbel. It was powered by rowing oars underwater. Though this craft was originally designed for underwater exploration, it did not take long for inventors and makers of war to recognize the military potential of the submersible. In 1648, Bishop John Wilkins wrote, “It may be of great advantage against a Navy of enemies, who by this may be undermined in the water and blown up.”","Over one hundred years later, the first military submarine was ready to be deployed. The Turtle was the world’s first submarine used in combat. Designed by David Bushnell in 1775, it was deployed by the Continental Army during the American Revolutionary War. Though the Turtle did utilize a support ship in combat, it was fully capable of renewing its air and power supplies independently; therefore, the Turtle is considered to have been a submarine and not a submersible.","The Turtle was powered by hand-cranked propellers and was named as such because it resembled a turtle. It held a single person, moved about three miles per hour in calm water, and contained enough air to stay submerged for about thirty minutes.","General Washington authorized an attack using the Turtle on a British flagship blockading New York Harbor. Sgt. Ezra Lee operated the Turtle and attempted to affix underwater explosives to the British ship, but he failed. The Turtle was later destroyed by the British. Despite this failure, the Turtle is still remembered as the first submarine used offensively during war. Another notable submarine originally designed for war was Julius H. Kroehl’s Submarine Explorer.","Built between 1863 and 1866, this submarine was created for the North during the American Civil War, but the war ended before it went into use. After the war, it was used commercially to harvest pearls in Panamanian waters during the late 1860s. Unfortunately, the dangers of decompression sickness (a condition that occurs when divers rise to the water’s surface too quickly) were not understood. While experimenting with the Submarine Explorer in 1867, Kroehl himself perished from decompression sickness.","In 1869, a new engineer put the Submarine Explorer back to the task of harvesting pearls. Tragically, the use of the Submarine Explorer was discontinued after the entire crew died from decompression sickness. Submarine use increased greatly during World War I. Due to innovations in engineering, such as a dual power system using both diesel and electric sources, submarines had finally developed into effective war machines.","One watercraft called the U-Boat was put to great effect by the Germans. Some argue that the U-Boat was more of a submersible, since U-Boats operated primarily on the surface using diesel engines and submerged only occasionally to attack using battery power, but the effectiveness of the U-Boat in combat is certain. During World War I, more than 5,000 Allied ships were sunk by UBoats, including the Lusitania, which is often cited as a reason why America entered the war.","UBoats were again utilized extensively by the Germans during World War II. Though the U-Boats were devastating to British fleets, advances in technology such as radar and sonar reduced their overall effectiveness. Additionally, the U.S. had also developed and deployed a fleet of submarines to great effect. Though the Japanese attack on Pearl Harbor destroyed or severely damaged many of the U.S. Navy’s frontline Pacific Fleet ships, U.S. submarines survived the attack and went on to cause great damage.","Submarines, though only about 2 percent of the U.S. Navy, destroyed over 30 percent of the Japanese Navy. This made U.S. submarines the most effective anti-ship and antisubmarine weapon in the entire American arsenal. Modern submarines are now powered by a nuclear reactor. The nuclear reactor generates a tremendous amount of power and frees the submarine from the need to occasionally surface."],"q":[["What does the passage imply as the primary reason submersibles like Drebbel's 1620 craft were limited compared to later submarines?",["Dependence on support for air and power renewal, restricting duration and size.","Lack of military recognition until Wilkins' 1648 comment.","Inability to resemble a turtle shape for camouflage.","Over-reliance on rowing oars instead of propellers."],"A",null],["Why does the passage classify the Turtle (1775) as a submarine despite using a support ship in combat?",["It resembled a turtle and held one person.","Its independent air and power renewal via hand-cranked propellers.","Success in sinking the British flagship.","Deployment by General Washington alone."],"B",null],["What interpretive lesson about early submarine risks emerges from the Submarine Explorer's pearl harvesting in the 1860s?",["War ended before effective use, so risks were irrelevant.","Ununderstood decompression sickness doomed Kroehl and crew despite postCivil War commercial shift.","Compressed air systems prevented all dangers.","Panama waters were too shallow for issues."],"B",null],["How does the passage interpret U-Boats' WWI success (over 5,000 Allied ships sunk) despite surface-primary operation?",["They were true submarines with unlimited battery power.","Lusitania sinking was accidental.","Dual diesel-electric system made them effective war machines, even if debated as submersibles","No Allied countermeasures existed."],"C",null],["What broader implication does the passage suggest about modern nuclear submarines from WWII U.S. effectiveness (30% Japanese Navy destruction)?",["Submarines remain 2% of fleets but dominate via reactors freeing surface needs.","Pearl Harbor proved surface vulnerability irrelevant.","U-Boats' radar defeats ended all submarine utility.","Smaller size always limits impact."],"A",null]]},{"id":"L6-10-11","label":"Post-test","title":"John Muir and the Sierra Club","focus":"Mixed","p":["In 1892, the Sierra Club was formed. In 1908, an area of coastal redwood trees north of San Francisco was established as Muir Woods National Monument. In the Sierra Nevada Mountains, a walking trail from Yosemite Valley to Mount Whitney was dedicated in 1938. It is called the John Muir Trail.","John Muir was born in 1838 in Scotland. His family name means “moor,” which is a meadow full of flowers and animals. John loved nature from the time he was small. He also liked to climb rocky cliffs and walls. When John was 11 years old, his family moved to the United States and settled in Wisconsin. John was good with tools and soon became an inventor. He first invented a model of a sawmill. Later, he invented an alarm clock that would cause the sleeping person to be tipped out of bed when the timer sounded.","Muir left home at an early age. He took a 1,000-mile walk south to the Gulf of Mexico in 1867 and 1868. Then he sailed for San Francisco. The city was too noisy and crowded for Muir, so he headed inland for the Sierra Nevadas.","When Muir discovered the Yosemite Valley in the Sierra Nevadas, it was as if he had come home. He loved the mountains, the wildlife, and the trees. He climbed the mountains and even climbed trees during thunderstorms in order to get closer to the wind. He put forth the theory in the late 1860s that the Yosemite Valley had been formed through the action of glaciers. People ridiculed him. Not until 1930 was Muir’s theory proven correct.","Muir began to write articles about the Yosemite Valley to tell readers about its beauty. His writing also warned people that Yosemite was in danger from timber mining and sheep ranching interests. In 1901, Theodore Roosevelt became president of the United States. He was interested in conservation. Muir took the president through Yosemite, and Roosevelt helped get legislation passed to create Yosemite National Park in 1906.","Although Muir won many conservation battles, he lost a major one. He fought to save the Hetch Hetchy Valley, which people wanted to dam in order to provide water for San Francisco. In late 1913, a bill was signed to dam the valley. Muir died in 1914. Some people say losing the fight to protect the valley killed Muir."],"q":[["In what year was the Sierra Club formed?",["1908","1892","1938","1914"],"B",null],["Where was John Muir born?",["Wisconsin","San Francisco","Scotland","Yosemite Valley"],"C",null],["Why did Muir leave San Francisco after arriving there?",["To visit the Gulf of Mexico","The city was too noisy and crowded","To invent new tools","To climb trees in thunderstorms"],"B",null],["What did Muir's alarm clock invention do?",["Saw wood automatically","Tipped the sleeping person out of bed","Measured glaciers","Warned of sheep ranching"],"B",null],["What does the text suggest Muir felt upon discovering Yosemite Valley?",["Fear of the wildlife","It was like coming home","Disappointment in the crowds","Boredom with the mountains"],"B",null],["Why was Muir's glacier theory initially ridiculed?",["It supported timber mining","People did not believe Yosemite was formed by glaciers","He climbed trees during storms","Roosevelt disagreed with it"],"B",null],["How did Muir's writing contribute to conservation?",["It described Yosemite's beauty and warned of dangers","It invented new trails","It promoted sheep ranching","It detailed his inventions only"],"A",null],["What major conservation loss is linked to Muir's death?",["Formation of the Sierra Club","Damming of Hetch Hetchy Valley","Dedication of the John Muir Trail","Roosevelt's Yosemite visit"],"B",null],["The text implies Muir's family name \"moor\" connected to his life how?",["His love for inventing tools","Early love for nature like meadows full of flowers and animals","Climbing rocky cliffs","Walking 1,000 miles south"],"B",null],["What does the damming of Hetch Hetchy Valley shortly after John Muir's death suggest about the limits of his influence on American conservation",["His inventions had no lasting environmental impact.","Despite his successes like founding the Sierra Club and promoting Yosemite, utilitarian demands for resources ultimately prevailed over pure preservation in key battles.","Sheep ranching was the primary threat he overlooked.","His glacier theory gained widespread acceptance too late."],"B",null]]}]};
